{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018svemSo1eYvYWa1ZVKkqKa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cursor-cell numbering review r4 — base design only\n2\t\n3\tReviewed HEAD `1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697`, the uncommitted round-4 note, report and base probe. Phase 6a reference: `d77da9f04276fca34db6fe6c36bea38a576c09b2`. Foreground review, no agents. Only this findings file was edited; experimental mutations used temporary in-memory Var replacements, restored in `finally`. JVM commands ran from `collab/numbering-probe`, supplying scripts on stdin to `mise exec -- java -Xmx1g` or `-Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -`.\n4\t\n5\t**No accepted-state canonicality counterexample was found.** The exact corrected independent 5,000-state generator numbered 4,110 states and structurally refused 890, with zero root or refusal-path mismatches over two independently renamed/shuffled copies per state. Independent exhaustive bijection checks verified 463 member swaps in 427 passing root-run tie groups. The base tie rule has a sound argument; D6's former recursive-search proof problem does not apply to this design.\n6\t\n7\t**Changes are still required for the resource gates and the probe's decoder ordering.** Comparison encoding now happens after a conservative byte charge, but the occurrence scan can overflow the host stack before the depth gate, and the representation estimator dropped 6a's early limit check. The probe also performs frame validation before discovery recomputation, so the unchanged 6a test run does not validate the claimed new decoder order.\n8\t\n9\tReferences: `note` = `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`; `probe` = `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`; `rows` and `rows_test` = phase 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`.\n10\t\n11\t| Severity | Location | Finding and required correction |\n12\t|---|---|---|\n13\t| **HIGH** | note:164–177, 249–256, 337–343; probe:121–150, 184–187, 392–403 | `occurrence-counts` runs the recursive `walk-cells` before the walk's depth check. With one reachable cell inside 3,000 nested vectors and `{:depth 3}`, numbering throws `StackOverflowError`, not `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:depth]`. The 4- and 300-level variants give the intended refusal. Enforce structural depth before recursive scans, memo-key hashing and spelling preparation can descend unchecked; carry depth through the scans or use a bounded iterative traversal. Add the deep adversarial case, not just `[[[[X]]]]`. The same discipline is needed for nested literal/descriptor representation sizing. |\n14\t| **MEDIUM** | note:168–178, 321–323; probe:94–110 | `rep-bound` is not the stated surviving 6a `size`: it removed the `remaining` argument and all intermediate limit checks. Under `{:work 2000}`, a set containing a 400,000-scalar literal invokes the estimator **400,012 times** before returning `[:work]`. CBOR allocation is correctly avoided, but the budget does not bound this preparation work or its recursion. Restore a remaining-budget-aware, early-aborting estimator, and qualify the accounting claim to distinguish encoded bytes, representation nodes and actual host heap bytes. A comparison byte vector and encoder intermediates consume more heap than the CBOR payload length; the representation bound is a conservative abstract charge, not a measured byte-for-byte heap ceiling. |\n15\t| **MEDIUM** | note:149–160, appendix A claim of exact implementation; probe:484–502 | The wrapper calls the entire old `rows/decode*`, including `validate-frames`, before doing discovery recomputation. The existing `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` witness, decoded with `p/decode*`, returns `:reserved-name` rather than `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Fix the probe or explicitly identify this second decoder limitation and add an actual discovery ordering test. The proposed production insertion around rows:570–575 is correctly placed before frame validation; preserve it rather than copying this wrapper literally. |\n16\t| **LOW** | note:270–274 | The asserted example of equal incomplete keys is stale after occurrence counts were added. A standalone `#{[A B] [B A]}` has counts 2,2; `#{[A B] [C D]}` has counts 1,1,1,1, and its disjoint members pass the local tie test. These are not the claimed equal residue keys. Replace the illustration with equal-size, equal-degree unresolved incidence structures, or remove that comparison. The incomplete-key clause itself remains necessary. |\n17\t| **LOW** | note:208–209, 249–256 | Make the termination measure precise for the queue as well as the current item. The prose explicitly allows a tie step that numbers no cell but replaces queued members with smaller sub-terms; that does not decrease the displayed pair when the walk's item remains the same. A multiset measure on pending subtree sizes, together with unnumbered-cell count and the explicit phase transitions, covers that case. Alternatively restrict that branch to invalid duplicate values and state the valid-input premise. This is a repairable proof presentation issue, not the former D6 recursion gap. |\n18\t| **LOW** | revise3 report, “What is refused, and why” | “The rest were harmless ties” is stronger than the measurements. The tables leave 60 refusals undecided (59 incidence-family states and one child state). Sampling equal roots is not proof of interchangeability. Preserve that distinction in the owner summary. |\n19\t\n20\t## 1. Canonicality and the independent generator\n21\t\n22\tI reused the corrected r3 script's definitions through `gen`, with seed `179221`, unchanged. Each iteration constructs the same two independent renamed/shuffled copies as r3, including all sets, maps and censuses. The removed D5/D6 comparisons consumed no randomness, so this preserves the original generator/copy sequence. Refusal classification uses a **separate** RNG (`9123`) and does not perturb that sequence.\n23\t\n24\tFor each state I compared the base `p/do-root` result across the three copies. Successful results must be the same root. Structural refusals were normalized to kind and row path, excluding source-name diagnostics. There were no resource refusals in this corpus.\n25\t\n26\t| Check | Result |\n27\t|---|---:|\n28\t| States / install-child states | 5,000 / 250 |\n29\t| Numbered | 4,110 |\n30\t| Structurally refused | 890 |\n31\t| Root or acceptance/refusal/path mismatches | **0** |\n32\t| Refused: exhaustively interchangeable | 470 |\n33\t| Refused: demonstrated non-interchangeable | 100 |\n34\t| Refused: sampled equal, undecided | 320 |\n35\t| Decode successes among first 100 states | 60 |\n36\t| Decode resource refusals in that subset | 14 |\n37\t| Other decode failures in that subset | **0** |\n38\t\n39\tThe first-100 decode check is a separate rerun preserving the same generator/copy sequence. The other 26 states were structurally refused. Resource refusals are the retained 6a recomputation limitation; successful valid carriers were checked through the base wrapper.\n40\t\n41\tMy refusal classifier does not call `p/classify-refused`, `p/reorder`, the author's permutations or renamer. It traverses the schema independently, transforms children before their parent, and records each guest set/map's member count in that post-order. It then takes the Cartesian product of independently generated permutations when the product is at most 720. Reordering each original task in the same post-order makes the container ordinals stable even when parents are shuffled. Above 720, it uses 48 independent shuffled arrangements. Permissive emission is used only to measure roots. Two different roots prove non-interchangeability; one root from exhaustive enumeration certifies the tested host-order invariance; one root from sampling stays undecided.\n42\t\n43\tTo attack positive tie-test results independently, I temporarily wrapped `group-failure`. On each passing **root-run** group, I collected the unnumbered cell IDs directly from its member data, enumerated bijections between the first member's cells and each other member's cells, extended each candidate to a two-way swap, and tested the entire task under that swap. My equality projection treats guest sets as mathematical sets, maps as maps, census as an ID-to-body map, and ordered rows as vectors. It retains bodies, aliasing, fixed numbered cells and all ordered occurrences. All **463 pair checks in 427 passing groups** found a whole-task automorphism. Some passing groups occur in a root run which eventually refuses because of another group; the check covers those too. This supplements, rather than substitutes for, the accepted-root comparison.\n44\t\n45\tAn earlier exploratory run sampled 16 permissive copies using the generator's own RNG and obtained 4,100/900. That altered later generated states. It is not the exact r3-sequence rerun and is not counted in the table above.\n46\t\n47\t### Assessment of section 2\n48\t\n49\tThe root tie step is sound: equal **complete** spellings provide an index-matched bijection preserving ordered structure, unordered membership, literal data, body rank, occurrence count and already-numbered cells. Clauses (ii) and (iii), after the fixpoint, ensure that every swapped unnumbered cell is confined to its one member. Extending that bijection to a block permutation therefore preserves the whole state. Swapping blocks changes private source-to-number maps but preserves emitted rows. This is precisely the confinement obligation that the r1 orbit argument lacked.\n50\t\n51\tWithin a key walk, confinement applies to the item being keyed, not to the whole root. That is sufficient for a canonical key: the item also reads the global occurrence counts and body ranks as invariant cell colors. Equal complete spellings preserve those colors. The local first-occurrence index preserves the alias relation; omitting it is unsound, as the targeted mutant below demonstrates.\n52\t\n53\tCounts are structure-derived because the scan counts schema positions, not distinct host objects or source IDs; it excludes the census and separately scoped child tasks. Shuffling unordered containers cannot change those counts. Body ranks derive from sorted distinct structural labels. The first-occurrence index derives from the key walk's canonical decisions, with nested walks starting at the enclosing local counter. Its validity follows from the smaller-subterm key induction plus the interchangeable-tie argument, not from an assumed host-independent raw encounter order.\n54\t\n55\tUnlike D6, a nested key request is over an actual smaller member subterm. There are no synthetic component or forced-trial transitions to justify. Fixpoint passes that repeat number a cell; passing nonempty cell-bearing tie groups also number cells. A finite queue/subterm argument covers the remaining no-new-cell branch. The displayed termination measure needs the clarification listed above, but I found no infinite loop or base canonicality defect.\n56\t\n57\tDo not describe this test as uniquely “the cheapest sound test” without a cost model. Nor does the seven-cell example prove that **every** sound rule needs search: richer deterministic structural analysis can distinguish a triangle from a square. It proves that arbitrary tied-edge entry is unsafe and that this chosen cheap test correctly refuses it. That is enough for this base design.\n58\t\n59\t## 2. Mutants\n60\t\n61\tAll mutants ran without source edits. The first four were rerun over the exact saved 5,000-state corpus and its two copies. The byte-charge mutant used the targeted large-literal budget fixture.\n62\t\n63\t| Mutation | Rerun result | What catches it |\n64\t|---|---|---|\n65\t| Drop occurrence count using `:count-spelling false` | 2,649 numbered, 2,351 refused; **0 invariance mismatches**; 2,073 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Count-specific fixture pins and coverage expectations. This is a legitimate alternative rule with worse coverage/different roots, not inherently a canonicality failure. |\n66\t| Enter failing ties in host order using `:permissive true` | 5,000 numbered; **86 root mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. This switch affects key walks as well as the root. |\n67\t| Skip confinement by suppressing only `:shared-across-groups` failures | 4,496 numbered, 504 refused; **42 mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. Keep the complete-key and within-group checks intact for this mutation. |\n68\t| Drop local first-occurrence index from `[:u j rank count]` | 3,998 numbered, 1,002 refused; **0 invariance mismatches in this generator**; 388 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Corpus pins catch a change, but this generator alone misses the unsoundness. The targeted balanced-alias witness below gives two accepted roots. |\n69\t| Skip representation-byte charge in `key-bytes!` | Huge-literal fixture numbers under `{:work 2000}`, charged work **44**, whereas base refuses `[:work]` | The large-literal budget assertion. A canonicality-only rerun does not test accounting. |\n70\t\n71\tThe balanced-alias witness is\n72\t\n73\t```clojure\n74\t#{[X X Y Y] [Z W Z W]}\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\tAll four cells have equal bodies and occurrence count 2. The base gives one root under reversal, `5376f133…`. Dropping the index makes both complete keys equal and the disjoint/confinement clauses pass, although the alias patterns differ. Reversing the set's enumeration then changes the accepted root from `5376f133…` to `09115434…`. Add this witness to the alias-pattern acceptance test; pair-edge-only examples do not adequately test the index component when count spelling is present.\n78\t\n79\t## 3. Byte/work accounting and gates\n80\t\n81\tThe former principal encoding defect is fixed: every comparison encoding in the numbering procedure goes through `key-bytes!`, which calls `spend!` on the representation bound before calling `bytes-key`. Sorting caches encoded keys rather than repeatedly encoding from the comparator. Synthetic cell/tag/tie overhead is included in the bound of the eventual encoded spelling; spelling positions are charged as they are constructed. Input literals are retained by reference in spellings.\n82\t\n83\tIndependent instrumentation and gate checks:\n84\t\n85\t| Case | Observation |\n86\t|---|---|\n87\t| 400,000-scalar literal inside a set, default budget | `[:work]`; only the 135-byte body-label encoding occurred before refusal, **no huge-literal encoding** |\n88\t| Same set under `{:work 2000}` | `[:work]`, but estimator entered 400,012 times before refusal |\n89\t| Same set with byte charge removed | Accepted; work 44 |\n90\t| Two cells, descriptor length 128 | Work 3,689; charged comparison encodings total 367 bytes |\n91\t| Two cells, descriptor length 65,536 | Work 265,321; comparison encodings total 65,778 bytes; accepted |\n92\t| 2, 65 and 10,000 cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` | All refuse `[:cursor-cells]` |\n93\t| One cell, 3,000 or 10,000 nested vectors, `{:depth 3}` | `StackOverflowError` before the depth gate |\n94\t| Same vector construction at depth 4 or 300 | Intended `[:depth]` refusal |\n95\t| Nested singleton sets at depths 40, 80, 120, 180 and 240 | All stop at `[:work]` under default budgets |\n96\t| Empty census, singleton set holding the huge literal | `[:work]`, rather than the old numbering procedure's immediate `{}` |\n97\t\n98\tThe descriptor measurement is my two-cell/single-body fixture, not the report's four-cell/two-body fixture; their absolute work totals need not match. It confirms that descriptor bytes are now charged and that local rank spellings do not repeat the large descriptor.\n99\t\n100\tThe census gate is before ranks/counts/walking, as promised, but **after** building the `bodies` map (probe:392–393). If “first” is intended to prevent oversized census preparation allocation too, check the raw pair count before that materialization. This is also a residual in 6a, whose label preparation precedes its gate; retaining the old line is not by itself evidence of preallocation protection.\n101\t\n102\tHashing memo keys containing member data can also scan large input literals before a byte charge. The implementation should make the accounting promise about bounded, charged preparation explicit, rather than calling every read/allocation paid. In particular, the estimator's docstring “allocating nothing” is literally false for `str` and lazy `mapcat` scaffolding, though those are not the old giant-CBOR allocation defect.\n103\t\n104\tThe nested-singleton result is another practical cost to report: fairly shallow identity-bearing structure can exhaust the new work budget even with one cell. It is a resource refusal, not a structural tie refusal or canonicality failure. No polynomial claim is needed, but a production performance assessment should include this shape.\n105\t\n106\t## 4. Refusal predicate and decode\n107\t\n108\tThe operational predicate in §3 matches `resolve!`: re-key queued groups to a no-progress pass; refuse if **any** group is incomplete, overlaps internally, or shares an unnumbered cell with another remaining group. If every group passes, enter them and return to the fixpoint. Key walks leave residue rather than refusing. `group-failure` implements the three clauses in the listed priority; root refusal reports the first failing group's path and all failing groups' unnumbered cells. The corrected mixed-body and anchored examples no longer overstate what an ordered anchor certifies.\n109\t\n110\tThe incomplete-key illustration needs the count-aware correction above. The operational iff should also be read subject to resource limits and valid input: a budget can stop the procedure before it reaches a structural decision, and an unreachable census cell raises a totality defect after a successful walk.\n111\t\n112\tInstall translation is fixed and independently verified. A parent with install `'a` holding the 2-cycle gives lift refusal `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[12 a 15]`. A phase-6a-emitted carrier for that parent decoded through discovery gives `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, carried cells `[:yin.k/cc-1 :yin.k/cc-2]`. Work-limit refusals at lift and decode under `{:work 1}` remain `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`, untranslated.\n113\t\n114\tThe whole-discovery-emission catch correctly includes children and catches only the specified lift-side refusal. The separate frame-order defect is real: the prototype's retained decoder already validates frames before the catch/recomputation. Run the rewritten ordering test against the discovery decoder, not merely the unchanged copied rows namespace.\n115\t\n116\t## 5. Section 4 audit and portability\n117\t\n118\tThe probe's rows and rows_test files match the phase-6a worktree by SHA-1:\n119\t\n120\t- rows: `e02350337c1b48a8d02901e46499d4cd0ab90f19`\n121\t- rows_test: `d8aeed70b39a1f4770ba99fb699d06e6561a4128`\n122\t\n123\tThe named deletion/rewrite spans are accurate: label-bound helper 212–230, checked wrappers 245–251, label seed 252–255, encoding 257–284, positions/depth 285–325, classes/refinement/search 326–354, emission 181–197 and 358–372, decoder recomputation 570–575 followed by frame validation 576–578. The rewritten test locations 171–178, 307–328, 400–403 and 406–421 are also correct. The unreachable `base` assertion is now explicitly rewritten as a totality defect; reachable/custody literal cases stay valid.\n124\t\n125\tThe cut list correctly retains the census gate and relocates the depth gate, adds the synthetic entry descriptor, threads child paths, and distinguishes the two `bytes-key` signatures. Add the depth/preparation and early-bound corrections to that brief; the listed four-level depth fixture cannot detect the current failure.\n126\t\n127\tPortable `.cljc` concerns are accurately identified: replace the probe's `for` in `group-failure` for potentially long sequences, keep 6a's byte-array-taking `bytes-key` with an explicit charged encode call, and remove reflection used only by the scratch emitter/decoder. The JVM `^bytes`/`alength` representation branch also needs the existing host-conditional implementation from 6a. Recursion depth must protect **all** preparatory traversals; guarding only the main walk does not establish safe bounded recursion on any of the three hosts.\n128\t\n129\tPhase 6a's unchanged tests passed independently: **21 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures/errors**. This validates the copied reference, not a production integration or a discovery decoder test suite. No Node or Dart execution was performed.\n130\t\n131\t## 6. Size, difficulty and refusal policy\n132\t\n133\tThe procedure range 79–416 has **305 nonblank lines**, or **296 after excluding comment-only lines**. The comparable 6a helpers have approximately 136 nonblank/noncomment lines. Thus the report's underlying size comparison is credible, although “non-blank” is an imprecise label for its counting rule. Roughly 200 added/replacement rows lines versus 135 deleted is a reasonable planning estimate, not an implemented diff. Correct scan/depth/accounting and decode validation add some work beyond that estimate.\n134\t\n135\tThe base is conceptually simpler than D6 and avoids 6a's label growth and individualization search. It still requires recursive local walks, provisional and root numbering, memoization sensitive to numbering/counter, faithful alias spellings, deferred worklists, confinement scans, resource accounting and decoder integration. It is **larger code and a substantial implementation**, not merely sorting a vector once. The proof burden is better contained than D6's, but “one page” does not make the boundary/accounting obligations disappear. I would not promise a shorter implementation/review schedule from these measurements.\n136\t\n137\tThe measured random refusal rate is **0.95%** (19/2000); frame-bearing is **2%** (4/200). These are generator rates, not production evidence. The independent tie-heavy corpus refuses **17.8%**, including 470 exhaustively harmless states. Valid small shapes that 6a can carry now deterministically refuse. That is an explicit behavioral tradeoff of the adopted base rule, not a defect that this review recommends repairing with D6.\n138\t\n139\tI cannot certify those rates as acceptable for unrestricted production suspension/migration. They may be acceptable for an explicitly refusal-capable carrier if callers preserve the live task and handle the structured refusal, but the samples cannot establish operational reliability. Treat this as an owner-accepted capability limit pending a real snapshot corpus; do not describe the random/frame samples as demonstrating practical coverage. Retain the occurrence count: it materially improves coverage, and dropping it saves little implementation complexity.\n140\t\n141\tThe owner-selected base remains a defensible implementation direction. Before implementation approval, fix the preparatory depth gate and early budget estimator, reconcile the probe's validator order with the production brief, and correct the small proof/example/report overclaims. No search extension is needed to address these findings.\n142\t\n143\tVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabb63dd-c047-4e34-a210-01b6c179a82e","uuid":"b80f7b1f-f483-45ef-9d57-891b22b657d9","timestamp":"2026-10-12T12:09:37.305Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a/collab/numbering-ref/review-r4.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering review r4 — base design only\n\nReviewed HEAD `1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697`, the uncommitted round-4 note, report and base probe. Phase 6a reference: `d77da9f04276fca34db6fe6c36bea38a576c09b2`. Foreground review, no agents. Only this findings file was edited; experimental mutations used temporary in-memory Var replacements, restored in `finally`. JVM commands ran from `collab/numbering-probe`, supplying scripts on stdin to `mise exec -- java -Xmx1g` or `-Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -`.\n\n**No accepted-state canonicality counterexample was found.** The exact corrected independent 5,000-state generator numbered 4,110 states and structurally refused 890, with zero root or refusal-path mismatches over two independently renamed/shuffled copies per state. Independent exhaustive bijection checks verified 463 member swaps in 427 passing root-run tie groups. The base tie rule has a sound argument; D6's former recursive-search proof problem does not apply to this design.\n\n**Changes are still required for the resource gates and the probe's decoder ordering.** Comparison encoding now happens after a conservative byte charge, but the occurrence scan can overflow the host stack before the depth gate, and the representation estimator dropped 6a's early limit check. The probe also performs frame validation before discovery recomputation, so the unchanged 6a test run does not validate the claimed new decoder order.\n\nReferences: `note` = `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`; `probe` = `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`; `rows` and `rows_test` = phase 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`.\n\n| Severity | Location | Finding and required correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| **HIGH** | note:164–177, 249–256, 337–343; probe:121–150, 184–187, 392–403 | `occurrence-counts` runs the recursive `walk-cells` before the walk's depth check. With one reachable cell inside 3,000 nested vectors and `{:depth 3}`, numbering throws `StackOverflowError`, not `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:depth]`. The 4- and 300-level variants give the intended refusal. Enforce structural depth before recursive scans, memo-key hashing and spelling preparation can descend unchecked; carry depth through the scans or use a bounded iterative traversal. Add the deep adversarial case, not just `[[[[X]]]]`. The same discipline is needed for nested literal/descriptor representation sizing. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:168–178, 321–323; probe:94–110 | `rep-bound` is not the stated surviving 6a `size`: it removed the `remaining` argument and all intermediate limit checks. Under `{:work 2000}`, a set containing a 400,000-scalar literal invokes the estimator **400,012 times** before returning `[:work]`. CBOR allocation is correctly avoided, but the budget does not bound this preparation work or its recursion. Restore a remaining-budget-aware, early-aborting estimator, and qualify the accounting claim to distinguish encoded bytes, representation nodes and actual host heap bytes. A comparison byte vector and encoder intermediates consume more heap than the CBOR payload length; the representation bound is a conservative abstract charge, not a measured byte-for-byte heap ceiling. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:149–160, appendix A claim of exact implementation; probe:484–502 | The wrapper calls the entire old `rows/decode*`, including `validate-frames`, before doing discovery recomputation. The existing `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` witness, decoded with `p/decode*`, returns `:reserved-name` rather than `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Fix the probe or explicitly identify this second decoder limitation and add an actual discovery ordering test. The proposed production insertion around rows:570–575 is correctly placed before frame validation; preserve it rather than copying this wrapper literally. |\n| **LOW** | note:270–274 | The asserted example of equal incomplete keys is stale after occurrence counts were added. A standalone `#{[A B] [B A]}` has counts 2,2; `#{[A B] [C D]}` has counts 1,1,1,1, and its disjoint members pass the local tie test. These are not the claimed equal residue keys. Replace the illustration with equal-size, equal-degree unresolved incidence structures, or remove that comparison. The incomplete-key clause itself remains necessary. |\n| **LOW** | note:208–209, 249–256 | Make the termination measure precise for the queue as well as the current item. The prose explicitly allows a tie step that numbers no cell but replaces queued members with smaller sub-terms; that does not decrease the displayed pair when the walk's item remains the same. A multiset measure on pending subtree sizes, together with unnumbered-cell count and the explicit phase transitions, covers that case. Alternatively restrict that branch to invalid duplicate values and state the valid-input premise. This is a repairable proof presentation issue, not the former D6 recursion gap. |\n| **LOW** | revise3 report, “What is refused, and why” | “The rest were harmless ties” is stronger than the measurements. The tables leave 60 refusals undecided (59 incidence-family states and one child state). Sampling equal roots is not proof of interchangeability. Preserve that distinction in the owner summary. |\n\n## 1. Canonicality and the independent generator\n\nI reused the corrected r3 script's definitions through `gen`, with seed `179221`, unchanged. Each iteration constructs the same two independent renamed/shuffled copies as r3, including all sets, maps and censuses. The removed D5/D6 comparisons consumed no randomness, so this preserves the original generator/copy sequence. Refusal classification uses a **separate** RNG (`9123`) and does not perturb that sequence.\n\nFor each state I compared the base `p/do-root` result across the three copies. Successful results must be the same root. Structural refusals were normalized to kind and row path, excluding source-name diagnostics. There were no resource refusals in this corpus.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| States / install-child states | 5,000 / 250 |\n| Numbered | 4,110 |\n| Structurally refused | 890 |\n| Root or acceptance/refusal/path mismatches | **0** |\n| Refused: exhaustively interchangeable | 470 |\n| Refused: demonstrated non-interchangeable | 100 |\n| Refused: sampled equal, undecided | 320 |\n| Decode successes among first 100 states | 60 |\n| Decode resource refusals in that subset | 14 |\n| Other decode failures in that subset | **0** |\n\nThe first-100 decode check is a separate rerun preserving the same generator/copy sequence. The other 26 states were structurally refused. Resource refusals are the retained 6a recomputation limitation; successful valid carriers were checked through the base wrapper.\n\nMy refusal classifier does not call `p/classify-refused`, `p/reorder`, the author's permutations or renamer. It traverses the schema independently, transforms children before their parent, and records each guest set/map's member count in that post-order. It then takes the Cartesian product of independently generated permutations when the product is at most 720. Reordering each original task in the same post-order makes the container ordinals stable even when parents are shuffled. Above 720, it uses 48 independent shuffled arrangements. Permissive emission is used only to measure roots. Two different roots prove non-interchangeability; one root from exhaustive enumeration certifies the tested host-order invariance; one root from sampling stays undecided.\n\nTo attack positive tie-test results independently, I temporarily wrapped `group-failure`. On each passing **root-run** group, I collected the unnumbered cell IDs directly from its member data, enumerated bijections between the first member's cells and each other member's cells, extended each candidate to a two-way swap, and tested the entire task under that swap. My equality projection treats guest sets as mathematical sets, maps as maps, census as an ID-to-body map, and ordered rows as vectors. It retains bodies, aliasing, fixed numbered cells and all ordered occurrences. All **463 pair checks in 427 passing groups** found a whole-task automorphism. Some passing groups occur in a root run which eventually refuses because of another group; the check covers those too. This supplements, rather than substitutes for, the accepted-root comparison.\n\nAn earlier exploratory run sampled 16 permissive copies using the generator's own RNG and obtained 4,100/900. That altered later generated states. It is not the exact r3-sequence rerun and is not counted in the table above.\n\n### Assessment of section 2\n\nThe root tie step is sound: equal **complete** spellings provide an index-matched bijection preserving ordered structure, unordered membership, literal data, body rank, occurrence count and already-numbered cells. Clauses (ii) and (iii), after the fixpoint, ensure that every swapped unnumbered cell is confined to its one member. Extending that bijection to a block permutation therefore preserves the whole state. Swapping blocks changes private source-to-number maps but preserves emitted rows. This is precisely the confinement obligation that the r1 orbit argument lacked.\n\nWithin a key walk, confinement applies to the item being keyed, not to the whole root. That is sufficient for a canonical key: the item also reads the global occurrence counts and body ranks as invariant cell colors. Equal complete spellings preserve those colors. The local first-occurrence index preserves the alias relation; omitting it is unsound, as the targeted mutant below demonstrates.\n\nCounts are structure-derived because the scan counts schema positions, not distinct host objects or source IDs; it excludes the census and separately scoped child tasks. Shuffling unordered containers cannot change those counts. Body ranks derive from sorted distinct structural labels. The first-occurrence index derives from the key walk's canonical decisions, with nested walks starting at the enclosing local counter. Its validity follows from the smaller-subterm key induction plus the interchangeable-tie argument, not from an assumed host-independent raw encounter order.\n\nUnlike D6, a nested key request is over an actual smaller member subterm. There are no synthetic component or forced-trial transitions to justify. Fixpoint passes that repeat number a cell; passing nonempty cell-bearing tie groups also number cells. A finite queue/subterm argument covers the remaining no-new-cell branch. The displayed termination measure needs the clarification listed above, but I found no infinite loop or base canonicality defect.\n\nDo not describe this test as uniquely “the cheapest sound test” without a cost model. Nor does the seven-cell example prove that **every** sound rule needs search: richer deterministic structural analysis can distinguish a triangle from a square. It proves that arbitrary tied-edge entry is unsafe and that this chosen cheap test correctly refuses it. That is enough for this base design.\n\n## 2. Mutants\n\nAll mutants ran without source edits. The first four were rerun over the exact saved 5,000-state corpus and its two copies. The byte-charge mutant used the targeted large-literal budget fixture.\n\n| Mutation | Rerun result | What catches it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drop occurrence count using `:count-spelling false` | 2,649 numbered, 2,351 refused; **0 invariance mismatches**; 2,073 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Count-specific fixture pins and coverage expectations. This is a legitimate alternative rule with worse coverage/different roots, not inherently a canonicality failure. |\n| Enter failing ties in host order using `:permissive true` | 5,000 numbered; **86 root mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. This switch affects key walks as well as the root. |\n| Skip confinement by suppressing only `:shared-across-groups` failures | 4,496 numbered, 504 refused; **42 mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. Keep the complete-key and within-group checks intact for this mutation. |\n| Drop local first-occurrence index from `[:u j rank count]` | 3,998 numbered, 1,002 refused; **0 invariance mismatches in this generator**; 388 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Corpus pins catch a change, but this generator alone misses the unsoundness. The targeted balanced-alias witness below gives two accepted roots. |\n| Skip representation-byte charge in `key-bytes!` | Huge-literal fixture numbers under `{:work 2000}`, charged work **44**, whereas base refuses `[:work]` | The large-literal budget assertion. A canonicality-only rerun does not test accounting. |\n\nThe balanced-alias witness is\n\n```clojure\n#{[X X Y Y] [Z W Z W]}\n```\n\nAll four cells have equal bodies and occurrence count 2. The base gives one root under reversal, `5376f133…`. Dropping the index makes both complete keys equal and the disjoint/confinement clauses pass, although the alias patterns differ. Reversing the set's enumeration then changes the accepted root from `5376f133…` to `09115434…`. Add this witness to the alias-pattern acceptance test; pair-edge-only examples do not adequately test the index component when count spelling is present.\n\n## 3. Byte/work accounting and gates\n\nThe former principal encoding defect is fixed: every comparison encoding in the numbering procedure goes through `key-bytes!`, which calls `spend!` on the representation bound before calling `bytes-key`. Sorting caches encoded keys rather than repeatedly encoding from the comparator. Synthetic cell/tag/tie overhead is included in the bound of the eventual encoded spelling; spelling positions are charged as they are constructed. Input literals are retained by reference in spellings.\n\nIndependent instrumentation and gate checks:\n\n| Case | Observation |\n|---|---|\n| 400,000-scalar literal inside a set, default budget | `[:work]`; only the 135-byte body-label encoding occurred before refusal, **no huge-literal encoding** |\n| Same set under `{:work 2000}` | `[:work]`, but estimator entered 400,012 times before refusal |\n| Same set with byte charge removed | Accepted; work 44 |\n| Two cells, descriptor length 128 | Work 3,689; charged comparison encodings total 367 bytes |\n| Two cells, descriptor length 65,536 | Work 265,321; comparison encodings total 65,778 bytes; accepted |\n| 2, 65 and 10,000 cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` | All refuse `[:cursor-cells]` |\n| One cell, 3,000 or 10,000 nested vectors, `{:depth 3}` | `StackOverflowError` before the depth gate |\n| Same vector construction at depth 4 or 300 | Intended `[:depth]` refusal |\n| Nested singleton sets at depths 40, 80, 120, 180 and 240 | All stop at `[:work]` under default budgets |\n| Empty census, singleton set holding the huge literal | `[:work]`, rather than the old numbering procedure's immediate `{}` |\n\nThe descriptor measurement is my two-cell/single-body fixture, not the report's four-cell/two-body fixture; their absolute work totals need not match. It confirms that descriptor bytes are now charged and that local rank spellings do not repeat the large descriptor.\n\nThe census gate is before ranks/counts/walking, as promised, but **after** building the `bodies` map (probe:392–393). If “first” is intended to prevent oversized census preparation allocation too, check the raw pair count before that materialization. This is also a residual in 6a, whose label preparation precedes its gate; retaining the old line is not by itself evidence of preallocation protection.\n\nHashing memo keys containing member data can also scan large input literals before a byte charge. The implementation should make the accounting promise about bounded, charged preparation explicit, rather than calling every read/allocation paid. In particular, the estimator's docstring “allocating nothing” is literally false for `str` and lazy `mapcat` scaffolding, though those are not the old giant-CBOR allocation defect.\n\nThe nested-singleton result is another practical cost to report: fairly shallow identity-bearing structure can exhaust the new work budget even with one cell. It is a resource refusal, not a structural tie refusal or canonicality failure. No polynomial claim is needed, but a production performance assessment should include this shape.\n\n## 4. Refusal predicate and decode\n\nThe operational predicate in §3 matches `resolve!`: re-key queued groups to a no-progress pass; refuse if **any** group is incomplete, overlaps internally, or shares an unnumbered cell with another remaining group. If every group passes, enter them and return to the fixpoint. Key walks leave residue rather than refusing. `group-failure` implements the three clauses in the listed priority; root refusal reports the first failing group's path and all failing groups' unnumbered cells. The corrected mixed-body and anchored examples no longer overstate what an ordered anchor certifies.\n\nThe incomplete-key illustration needs the count-aware correction above. The operational iff should also be read subject to resource limits and valid input: a budget can stop the procedure before it reaches a structural decision, and an unreachable census cell raises a totality defect after a successful walk.\n\nInstall translation is fixed and independently verified. A parent with install `'a` holding the 2-cycle gives lift refusal `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[12 a 15]`. A phase-6a-emitted carrier for that parent decoded through discovery gives `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, carried cells `[:yin.k/cc-1 :yin.k/cc-2]`. Work-limit refusals at lift and decode under `{:work 1}` remain `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`, untranslated.\n\nThe whole-discovery-emission catch correctly includes children and catches only the specified lift-side refusal. The separate frame-order defect is real: the prototype's retained decoder already validates frames before the catch/recomputation. Run the rewritten ordering test against the discovery decoder, not merely the unchanged copied rows namespace.\n\n## 5. Section 4 audit and portability\n\nThe probe's rows and rows_test files match the phase-6a worktree by SHA-1:\n\n- rows: `e02350337c1b48a8d02901e46499d4cd0ab90f19`\n- rows_test: `d8aeed70b39a1f4770ba99fb699d06e6561a4128`\n\nThe named deletion/rewrite spans are accurate: label-bound helper 212–230, checked wrappers 245–251, label seed 252–255, encoding 257–284, positions/depth 285–325, classes/refinement/search 326–354, emission 181–197 and 358–372, decoder recomputation 570–575 followed by frame validation 576–578. The rewritten test locations 171–178, 307–328, 400–403 and 406–421 are also correct. The unreachable `base` assertion is now explicitly rewritten as a totality defect; reachable/custody literal cases stay valid.\n\nThe cut list correctly retains the census gate and relocates the depth gate, adds the synthetic entry descriptor, threads child paths, and distinguishes the two `bytes-key` signatures. Add the depth/preparation and early-bound corrections to that brief; the listed four-level depth fixture cannot detect the current failure.\n\nPortable `.cljc` concerns are accurately identified: replace the probe's `for` in `group-failure` for potentially long sequences, keep 6a's byte-array-taking `bytes-key` with an explicit charged encode call, and remove reflection used only by the scratch emitter/decoder. The JVM `^bytes`/`alength` representation branch also needs the existing host-conditional implementation from 6a. Recursion depth must protect **all** preparatory traversals; guarding only the main walk does not establish safe bounded recursion on any of the three hosts.\n\nPhase 6a's unchanged tests passed independently: **21 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures/errors**. This validates the copied reference, not a production integration or a discovery decoder test suite. No Node or Dart execution was performed.\n\n## 6. Size, difficulty and refusal policy\n\nThe procedure range 79–416 has **305 nonblank lines**, or **296 after excluding comment-only lines**. The comparable 6a helpers have approximately 136 nonblank/noncomment lines. Thus the report's underlying size comparison is credible, although “non-blank” is an imprecise label for its counting rule. Roughly 200 added/replacement rows lines versus 135 deleted is a reasonable planning estimate, not an implemented diff. Correct scan/depth/accounting and decode validation add some work beyond that estimate.\n\nThe base is conceptually simpler than D6 and avoids 6a's label growth and individualization search. It still requires recursive local walks, provisional and root numbering, memoization sensitive to numbering/counter, faithful alias spellings, deferred worklists, confinement scans, resource accounting and decoder integration. It is **larger code and a substantial implementation**, not merely sorting a vector once. The proof burden is better contained than D6's, but “one page” does not make the boundary/accounting obligations disappear. I would not promise a shorter implementation/review schedule from these measurements.\n\nThe measured random refusal rate is **0.95%** (19/2000); frame-bearing is **2%** (4/200). These are generator rates, not production evidence. The independent tie-heavy corpus refuses **17.8%**, including 470 exhaustively harmless states. Valid small shapes that 6a can carry now deterministically refuse. That is an explicit behavioral tradeoff of the adopted base rule, not a defect that this review recommends repairing with D6.\n\nI cannot certify those rates as acceptable for unrestricted production suspension/migration. They may be acceptable for an explicitly refusal-capable carrier if callers preserve the live task and handle the structured refusal, but the samples cannot establish operational reliability. Treat this as an owner-accepted capability limit pending a real snapshot corpus; do not describe the random/frame samples as demonstrating practical coverage. Retain the occurrence count: it materially improves coverage, and dropping it saves little implementation complexity.\n\nThe owner-selected base remains a defensible implementation direction. Before implementation approval, fix the preparatory depth gate and early budget estimator, reconcile the probe's validator order with the production brief, and correct the small proof/example/report overclaims. No search extension is needed to address these findings.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n","numLines":144,"startLine":1,"totalLines":144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018svemSo1eYvYWa1ZVKkqKa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxKR8XHChZ1T8EdjEUrc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01341qH3FjFmkv7q2pf6T2nV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a/collab/numbering-ref/probe/src/probe/discovery.clj"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29674,"cache_read_input_tokens":31522,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29674},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabb63dd-c047-4e34-a210-01b6c179a82e","uuid":"a7fb4191-87f1-4202-b00e-03e09040a3e3","timestamp":"2026-10-12T12:09:37.789Z","request_id":"req_011CfxKR8DgnNAbXoA2i8UQq","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01341qH3FjFmkv7q2pf6T2nV":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a/collab/numbering-ref/probe/src/probe/discovery.clj"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01341qH3FjFmkv7q2pf6T2nV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns probe.discovery\n2\t  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering, the BASE design of the\n3\t   note of 2026-10-12 (round 4), over the 6a abstract task representation\n4\t   (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/. The\n5\t   round-3 variants (components, residue search) live in probe.discovery-r3.\n6\t\n7\t   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n8\t   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n9\t\n10\t   The rule. A cursor cell gets the next number the first time the walk meets\n11\t   it. The walk is pre-order over the task in table order. A guest :set or\n12\t   :map is turned into a vector of its members (a map entry as [:entry k v])\n13\t   sorted by KEY: the spelling of a local walk over the member under the\n14\t   current numbering, with numbered cells spelled by number and unnumbered\n15\t   ones by local first-occurrence index, body rank and occurrence count.\n16\t   Members whose keys tie are POSTPONED to the run's queue and re-keyed after\n17\t   the rest of the walk, until a pass numbers nothing. A remaining tie group\n18\t   is entered in listed order when the TIE TEST holds (common key complete;\n19\t   the members' unnumbered cells pairwise disjoint and occurring in no member\n20\t   of another remaining group); otherwise the root walk refuses\n21\t   :yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells and a key walk spells the\n22\t   group as [:tie K ... K] in its key.\n23\t\n24\t   Budgets: :cursor-cells (the census's pair count, before anything is\n25\t   built), :depth (enforced by one bounded iterative scan of the task before\n26\t   any recursive traversal, and again in the walk), :work (one unit per\n27\t   position scanned, visited, keyed or spelled, plus 6a's representation\n28\t   bound of every value before it is CBOR-encoded for a comparison, computed\n29\t   iteratively against the remaining budget). Exceeding one is\n30\t   :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\n31\t\n32\t   Diagnostic switches (*numbering-opts*): :permissive enters a failing group\n33\t   in listed order instead of refusing (used to test interchangeability by\n34\t   exhaustion or sampling); :count-spelling false drops the occurrence count\n35\t   from the key (the owner's literal direction, for measurement).\"\n36\t  (:require [clojure.set :as set]\n37\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n38\t            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n39\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n40\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n41\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n42\t\n43\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n44\t\n45\t(def synthetic-table\n46\t  \"The one row that exists only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an\n47\t   ordered pair, so the alias pattern across key and value is in its key.\"\n48\t  {:entry [:V :V]})\n49\t\n50\t(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n51\t  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n52\t    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n53\t          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n54\t          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n55\t          :else schema)))\n56\t\n57\t(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n58\t(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n59\t(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n60\t\n61\t(defn bytes-key\n62\t  \"Uncharged encoding; generators and diagnostics only.\"\n63\t  [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n64\t\n65\t(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n66\t  (loop [i 0]\n67\t    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n68\t          (= i (count b)) 1\n69\t          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n70\t          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n71\t\n72\t(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n73\t\n74\t(defn elem-item\n75\t  \"The item a member is keyed and entered as: a :set member is its V; a :map\n76\t   entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v].\"\n77\t  [kind e]\n78\t  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n79\t\n80\t(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n81\t\n82\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n83\t\n84\t(def default-budget\n85\t  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes and :search; :work now also counts\n86\t   representation bytes, so its default takes over :label-bytes' scale.\"\n87\t  (assoc (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes :search) :work 4194304))\n88\t\n89\t(defn- limit! [ctx category]\n90\t  (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n91\t                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]\n92\t                   ;; probe-only diagnostic (counters at the refusal), not part of the contract\n93\t                   :probe/used @(:counter ctx)})))\n94\t\n95\t(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n96\t  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n97\t    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category)) (limit! ctx category))))\n98\t\n99\t(defn- charge-rep-bound!\n100\t  \"6a's conservative representation bound of x (check-label-bytes!'s `size`:\n101\t   128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length per byte\n102\t   payload, metadata included), charged to :work. Computed with an explicit\n103\t   stack, no recursion, against the budget REMAINING at the call: the running\n104\t   total is checked after every node, so the estimator stops after at most\n105\t   remaining/16 steps on any input and never finishes reading a value it\n106\t   cannot afford. The bound is an abstract charge: an upper bound of the\n107\t   encoding's bytes and of the encoder's intermediate nodes, not a measured\n108\t   host-heap ceiling (the comparison vector and the encoder's intermediates\n109\t   use more heap than the payload length). Reading x allocates the lazy\n110\t   element sequences and `str` of names; nothing proportional to the\n111\t   encoding.\"\n112\t  [ctx x]\n113\t  (let [remaining (- (get (:budget ctx) :work) (get @(:counter ctx) :work 0))]\n114\t    (loop [stack (list (list x)) total 0 steps 0]\n115\t      (if (empty? stack)\n116\t        (do (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) steps)\n117\t            (spend! ctx :work total))\n118\t        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n119\t          (if (empty? s)\n120\t            (recur stack total steps)\n121\t            (let [x (first s)\n122\t                  stack (cons (rest s) stack)\n123\t                  n (cond (cbor/byte-payload? x) (+ 16 (alength ^bytes x))\n124\t                          (string? x) (+ 128 (* 4 (count x)))\n125\t                          (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) (+ 128 (* 4 (count (str x))))\n126\t                          :else 128)\n127\t                  total (+ total n)]\n128\t              (when (> total remaining)\n129\t                (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) (inc steps))\n130\t                (limit! ctx :work))\n131\t              (recur (cond->> stack\n132\t                       (coll? x) (cons (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) (seq x)))\n133\t                       (meta x) (cons (list (meta x))))\n134\t                     total (inc steps)))))))))\n135\t\n136\t(defn- key-bytes!\n137\t  \"Charge the representation bound of x to :work, then encode x for comparison.\n138\t   Every encoding the procedure makes goes through here.\"\n139\t  [ctx x]\n140\t  (charge-rep-bound! ctx x)\n141\t  (let [bs (bytes-key x)]\n142\t    (swap! (:counter ctx) update :encoded-bytes (fnil + 0) (count bs))\n143\t    bs))\n144\t\n145\t(defn- depth-scan!\n146\t  \"The :depth gate, enforced BEFORE any recursive traversal of the task: one\n147\t   bounded pre-order scan with an explicit stack (no recursion) over rows,\n148\t   members, structural and literal data and the census; install children are\n149\t   skipped (each is gated by its own procedure). The depth of a position is\n150\t   the walk's measure, its slot steps from the task (a member step counts\n151\t   one, a map entry's key and value two), plus, inside data, its collection\n152\t   nesting (metadata counts one level). A position deeper than :depth refuses\n153\t   [:depth]; every position costs one :work unit. After the scan every\n154\t   recursive read of the task (cell scans, memo-key hashing, spelling,\n155\t   encoding, 6a's emitter) nests at most :depth levels.\"\n156\t  [ctx task]\n157\t  (let [limit (get (:budget ctx) :depth)\n158\t        node (fn [n k] [:node nil n k])\n159\t        slot (fn [d v k] [:slot d v k])\n160\t        data (fn [v k] [:data nil v k])]\n161\t    ;; The stack holds lazy sequences of entries, so pushing a container's\n162\t    ;; elements is O(1): every position is charged when it is popped, before\n163\t    ;; any work proportional to it is done.\n164\t    (loop [stack (list (list (node task 0)))]\n165\t      (when (seq stack)\n166\t        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n167\t          (if (empty? s)\n168\t            (recur stack)\n169\t            (let [[tag d x k] (first s) stack (cons (rest s) stack)]\n170\t              (spend! ctx :work 1)\n171\t              (when (> k limit) (limit! ctx :depth))\n172\t              (recur\n173\t               (cons\n174\t                (case tag\n175\t                  :node\n176\t                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} x k' (inc k)]\n177\t                    (if (child-task? ctx x)\n178\t                      nil\n179\t                      (let [ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n180\t                        (cond\n181\t                          (= :task kind)\n182\t                          (mapcat (fn [i d v]\n183\t                                    (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n184\t                                      (map (fn [child] (node child (inc k'))) v)\n185\t                                      [(slot d v k')]))\n186\t                                  (range) ds slots)\n187\t                          (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n188\t                          (mapcat (fn [[key v]] [(data key k') (slot (second ds) v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n189\t                          (= :window kind)\n190\t                          (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [(data r k') (slot :V v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n191\t                          (= :set kind) (map (fn [v] (slot :V v k')) slots)\n192\t                          (= :map kind)\n193\t                          (mapcat (fn [[a b]] [(slot :V a (inc k')) (slot :V b (inc k'))]) (partition 2 slots))\n194\t                          :else (map (fn [d v] (slot d v k')) ds slots)))))\n195\t                  :slot\n196\t                  (cond (ref? d) [(node x k)]\n197\t                        (refs? d) (map (fn [n] (node n (inc k))) x)\n198\t                        (= :C d) nil\n199\t                        (v? d) (when x\n200\t                                 (case (first x)\n201\t                                   :cursor-cell nil\n202\t                                   :row [(node (second x) k)]\n203\t                                   :lit [(data (second x) k)]\n204\t                                   [(data x k)]))\n205\t                        :else [(data x k)])\n206\t                  :data\n207\t                  (concat (when (coll? x) (map (fn [e] (data e (inc k))) (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) x)))\n208\t                          (when (meta x) [(data (meta x) (inc k))])))\n209\t                stack)))))))))\n210\t\n211\t(defn- sort-by-key!\n212\t  \"xs in the bytewise order of (f x), each encoded once (stable).\"\n213\t  [ctx f xs]\n214\t  (mapv second (sort #(compare-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) (mapv (fn [x] [(key-bytes! ctx (f x)) x]) xs))))\n215\t\n216\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ cell scans\n217\t\n218\t(defn- walk-cells\n219\t  \"Call f on every cursor cell at a position under slot (d x); census and\n220\t   install children excluded; one :work unit per position.\"\n221\t  [ctx d x f]\n222\t  (letfn [(slot [d x]\n223\t            (spend! ctx :work 1)\n224\t            (cond (ref? d) (node x)\n225\t                  (refs? d) (run! node x)\n226\t                  (= :C d) (f x)\n227\t                  (v? d) (when x (case (first x) :cursor-cell (f (second x)) :row (node (second x)) nil))\n228\t                  :else nil))\n229\t          (node [n]\n230\t            (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (child-task? ctx n))\n231\t              (dorun (map slot (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n)))))]\n232\t    (slot d x)))\n233\t\n234\t(defn item-cells\n235\t  \"The cursor cells under slot (d x), memoized per procedure on the slot value.\"\n236\t  [ctx d x]\n237\t  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\n238\t      (let [acc (atom #{})]\n239\t        (walk-cells ctx d x #(swap! acc conj %))\n240\t        (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\n241\t        @acc)))\n242\t\n243\t(defn- occurrence-counts\n244\t  \"Cursor cell -> number of positions of the task holding it.\"\n245\t  [ctx task]\n246\t  (let [acc (atom {})]\n247\t    (walk-cells ctx [:ref :task] task #(swap! acc update % (fnil inc 0)))\n248\t    @acc))\n249\t\n250\t(defn body-label [body]\n251\t  (let [[stream position] (:slots body)]\n252\t    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n253\t\n254\t(defn- body-ranks\n255\t  \"Cursor cell -> rank of its body label among the census's distinct body\n256\t   labels in bytewise order (rank order = label order, so keys stay\n257\t   independent of the descriptor's size).\"\n258\t  [ctx bodies]\n259\t  (let [labels (into {} (map (fn [[c b]] [c (body-label b)])) bodies)\n260\t        ranks (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i l] [l i])) (sort-by-key! ctx identity (distinct (vals labels))))]\n261\t    (into {} (map (fn [[c l]] [c (get ranks l)])) labels)))\n262\t\n263\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\n264\t\n265\t(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\n266\t\n267\t(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next]\n268\t  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next) :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\n269\t\n270\t(defn- meet!\n271\t  \"W2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\"\n272\t  [run c]\n273\t  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\n274\t    (let [k @(:next run)]\n275\t      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\n276\t      (swap! (:next run) inc)\n277\t      true)))\n278\t\n279\t(declare enter-node! unordered! local-run spell-slot spell-node)\n280\t\n281\t(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\n282\t  (let [ctx (:ctx run)]\n283\t    (spend! ctx :work 1)\n284\t    (when (> (count path) (get (:budget ctx) :depth)) (limit! ctx :depth))\n285\t    (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\n286\t          (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\n287\t          (= :C d) (meet! run x)\n288\t          (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n289\t                           :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\n290\t                           :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\n291\t                           nil))\n292\t          :else nil)))\n293\t\n294\t(defn- enter-node!\n295\t  \"W1. Pre-order: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in order;\n296\t   keyed structural rows in the byte order of the key; :window in register\n297\t   order; installs and module stores by name; :set and :map by keys with\n298\t   ties postponed; census and children not entered.\"\n299\t  [run n path]\n300\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n301\t    (cond\n302\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n303\t      (child-task? ctx n) nil\n304\t      (= :task kind)\n305\t      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n306\t        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n307\t          (doseq [child (sort-by-key! ctx #(first (:slots %)) x)]\n308\t            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\n309\t          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\n310\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n311\t      (doseq [[k v] (sort-by-key! ctx first (partition 2 slots))]\n312\t        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\n313\t      (= :window kind)\n314\t      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\n315\t      (= :set kind) (unordered! run n :set (vec slots) path)\n316\t      (= :map kind) (unordered! run n :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\n317\t      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n318\t\n319\t(defn- unordered!\n320\t  \"The transformation: key every member by a local walk under the numbering\n321\t   at this moment; members with a unique key are entered in key order; a run\n322\t   of equal keys is one tie group, postponed to the run's queue (postponement\n323\t   order: containers in walk order, groups within a container in key order).\n324\t   A member's path is the container's path plus :member (there is no\n325\t   canonical index into an unordered container); a group remembers its\n326\t   container by identity.\"\n327\t  [run container kind elems path]\n328\t  (let [keyed (mapv (fn [e]\n329\t                      ;; one unit per member keyed, so the number of key walks\n330\t                      ;; (and of memo lookups, each a read of charged data) is charged\n331\t                      (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n332\t                      [(local-run run (elem-item kind e) (conj path :member)) e]) elems)\n333\t        groups (partition-by (comp :bytes first) (sort #(compare-bytes (:bytes (first %1)) (:bytes (first %2))) keyed))]\n334\t    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n335\t      (println \"  [sort]\" path \"num\" @(:num run))\n336\t      (doseq [g groups] (println \"    \" (if (= 1 (count g)) \"enter\" \"POSTPONE\") (mapv (fn [[r e]] [e (:spell r)]) g))))\n337\t    (doseq [g groups]\n338\t      (if (= 1 (count g))\n339\t        (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind (second (first g))) (conj path :member))\n340\t        (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :container container :kind kind :path path\n341\t                                   :key (:spell (first (first g))) :complete? (:complete? (first (first g)))\n342\t                                   :elems (mapv second g)})))))\n343\t\n344\t(defn- fixpoint!\n345\t  \"W3. Re-examine every postponed group in order under the current numbering;\n346\t   repeat while a pass numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\"\n347\t  [run]\n348\t  (loop []\n349\t    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\n350\t      (reset! (:groups run) [])\n351\t      (doseq [{:keys [container kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run container kind elems path))\n352\t      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\n353\t\n354\t(defn- unnumbered-cells [run kind e]\n355\t  (let [num @(:num run)]\n356\t    (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells (:ctx run) :V (elem-item kind e))))))\n357\t\n358\t(defn- group-failure\n359\t  \"T, the tie test. nil when the group's members are interchangeable: the\n360\t   common key is complete, and the members' unnumbered cursor cells are\n361\t   pairwise disjoint and occur in no member of another remaining group.\n362\t   Otherwise the reason.\"\n363\t  [run groups g]\n364\t  (let [own (mapv #(unnumbered-cells run (:kind g) %) (:elems g))\n365\t        others (reduce into #{} (for [h groups :when (not= (:id h) (:id g)) e (:elems h)]\n366\t                                  (unnumbered-cells run (:kind h) e)))]\n367\t    (cond (not (:complete? g)) :incomplete-key\n368\t          (not= (reduce + (map count own)) (count (reduce into #{} own))) :shared-within-group\n369\t          (some #(seq (set/intersection % others)) own) :shared-across-groups\n370\t          :else nil)))\n371\t\n372\t(defn- resolve!\n373\t  \"Postponed groups to completion: fixpoint, then the tie test on every\n374\t   remaining group; all pass: enter them in listed order and repeat; any\n375\t   fails: the root run refuses, a key run leaves them as residue.\"\n376\t  [run]\n377\t  (loop []\n378\t    (fixpoint! run)\n379\t    (let [groups @(:groups run) ctx (:ctx run)]\n380\t      (when (seq groups)\n381\t        (let [failing (vec (keep (fn [g] (when-let [why (group-failure run groups g)] (assoc g :why why))) groups))]\n382\t          (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n383\t            (println \"  [ties]\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (:elems g) (:why g)]) (map #(assoc % :why (group-failure run groups %)) groups))))\n384\t          (cond\n385\t            (empty? failing)\n386\t            ;; Interchangeable groups are independent of each other (their\n387\t            ;; cells are confined), so all are entered before the next pass.\n388\t            (do (reset! (:groups run) [])\n389\t                (doseq [g groups e (:elems g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item (:kind g) e) (conj (:path g) :member)))\n390\t                (recur))\n391\t            (:permissive ctx)\n392\t            ;; Diagnostic only: enter the first group in listed order, then\n393\t            ;; re-key the rest (what an unsound \"equal keys, host order\" rule does).\n394\t            (do (reset! (:groups run) (vec (rest groups)))\n395\t                (doseq [e (:elems (first groups))] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item (:kind (first groups)) e) (conj (:path (first groups)) :member)))\n396\t                (recur))\n397\t            (= :n (:tag run))\n398\t            (reset! (:refused run)\n399\t                    {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n400\t                     :path (:path (first failing))\n401\t                     :why (mapv :why failing)\n402\t                     :cursor-cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat (fn [g] (mapcat #(unnumbered-cells run (:kind g) %) (:elems g))) failing))))})\n403\t            :else nil))))))\n404\t\n405\t(defn- local-run\n406\t  \"A key walk: the same procedure on one member from the enclosing run's\n407\t   numbering, tag :u; its result is the member's key (spelling under its\n408\t   final numbering, residue spelled [:tie K ...]) and whether it is complete.\n409\t   Memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the item's cells, counter).\"\n410\t  [run item path]\n411\t  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\n412\t        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\n413\t        num @(:num run)\n414\t        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\n415\t        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start]]\n416\t    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\n417\t        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start)]\n418\t          (spend! ctx :work (count cells))\n419\t          (enter-slot! sub :V item path)\n420\t          (resolve! sub)\n421\t          (let [spell (spell-slot sub :V item path)\n422\t                r {:spell spell :bytes (key-bytes! ctx spell) :complete? (empty? @(:groups sub))}]\n423\t            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :local-runs (fnil + 0) 1)\n424\t            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\n425\t            r)))))\n426\t\n427\t;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\n428\t\n429\t(defn- spell-cell [run c]\n430\t  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c) ctx (:ctx run)]\n431\t    (case t\n432\t      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\n433\t      :u [:cursor-cell (if (:count-spelling ctx)\n434\t                         [:u k (get (:ranks ctx) c) (get (:counts ctx) c)]\n435\t                         [:u k (get (:ranks ctx) c)])]\n436\t      (throw (ex-info \"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\n437\t\n438\t(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\n439\t  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n440\t  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n441\t        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n442\t        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n443\t        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n444\t                   (case (first x)\n445\t                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\n446\t                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\n447\t                     x))\n448\t        :else x))\n449\t\n450\t(defn- spell-node\n451\t  \"The spelling of a row under the run's final numbering: literals and\n452\t   structural data as themselves (by reference), rows expanded in place,\n453\t   keyed rows in key order, a :set or :map as the sorted vector of its\n454\t   members' spellings with each surviving tie group as [:tie K ...].\"\n455\t  [run n path]\n456\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n457\t    (cond\n458\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n459\t      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\n460\t      (= :task kind)\n461\t      (into [kind]\n462\t            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\n463\t                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n464\t                             (sort-by-key! ctx #(nth % 1) (mapv #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x))\n465\t                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\n466\t                         (map vector ds slots)))\n467\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n468\t      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (sort-by-key! ctx first\n469\t                                                  (mapv (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\n470\t                                                        (partition 2 slots)))))\n471\t      (= :window kind)\n472\t      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n473\t      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\n474\t      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n475\t            tied (filter #(identical? n (:container %)) @(:groups run))\n476\t            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\n477\t            plain (keep (fn [e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path :member)))) elems)\n478\t            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\n479\t        (into [kind] (sort-by-key! ctx identity (concat plain ties))))\n480\t      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n481\t\n482\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\n483\t\n484\t(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts*\n485\t  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install\n486\t   children): :permissive, :count-spelling, :budget.\"\n487\t  {})\n488\t\n489\t(defn discovery-numbering\n490\t  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n491\t   Throws :resource-limit when a budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n492\t   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n493\t  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n494\t  ([task opts]\n495\t   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n496\t         ctx {:task task\n497\t              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n498\t              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n499\t              :counter (atom {})\n500\t              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n501\t              :count-spelling (not= false (:count-spelling opts))}\n502\t         ;; gates first: the census's pair count before the bodies map is\n503\t         ;; built, the depth scan before any recursive traversal\n504\t         _ (spend! ctx :cursor-cells (quot (count (:slots census)) 2))\n505\t         _ (depth-scan! ctx task)\n506\t         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n507\t         ctx (assoc ctx :bodies bodies)\n508\t         ctx (assoc ctx :ranks (body-ranks ctx bodies) :counts (occurrence-counts ctx task))\n509\t         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1)]\n510\t     (enter-node! run task [])\n511\t     (resolve! run)\n512\t     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n513\t       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n514\t       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n515\t         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n516\t         (when (not= numbered declared)\n517\t           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (sort (remove numbered declared)))\n518\t                                                      :extra (vec (sort (remove declared numbered)))})))\n519\t         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n520\t          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n521\t\n522\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n523\t;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n524\t;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n525\t\n526\t(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n527\t(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n528\t(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n529\t\n530\t(declare emit-node*)\n531\t\n532\t(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\n533\t  (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n534\t                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n535\t                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)\n536\t                   ;; probe-only diagnostic, not part of the refusal's contract\n537\t                   :probe/why (:why r)})))\n538\t\n539\t(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows path]\n540\t  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n541\t    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n542\t  (cond\n543\t    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows path)\n544\t    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] [j n]]\n545\t                        (let [p (conj path (if (= :install (:kind n)) (first (:slots n)) j))\n546\t                              [id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs p)]\n547\t                          [(conj out id) rs']))\n548\t                      [[] rows] (map-indexed vector x))\n549\t    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n550\t    (v? d) (case (first x)\n551\t             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows path)] [[:row id] rs])\n552\t             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n553\t             [x rows])\n554\t    :else [x rows]))\n555\t\n556\t(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows path]\n557\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n558\t        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [i d x]]\n559\t                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n560\t                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x)\n561\t                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) path))\n562\t                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {} [])]\n563\t                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n564\t                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs (conj path i))] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n565\t                             [[] rows] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n566\t        slots (if (= kind :task)\n567\t                (-> slots\n568\t                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n569\t                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n570\t                (ordered-slots kind slots))\n571\t        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\n572\t    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\n573\t\n574\t(defn emit*\n575\t  \"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\n576\t   :unresolved-cursor-cells for the root or for any install child (path\n577\t   [12 <name> ...]); a budget refusal propagates as itself.\"\n578\t  [task]\n579\t  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n580\t    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\n581\t    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {} [])]\n582\t      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\n583\t\n584\t(defn decode*\n585\t  \"Receiver side, in the production order (rows.cljc:570-578): 6a's decode*\n586\t   steps 1-5 with its own numbering check off and its frame validation\n587\t   suppressed (it still runs 6a's recompute under 6a's budget: a probe\n588\t   limitation), then the whole discovery recomputation (root and install\n589\t   children) under one translation: only a lift-side :yin.k/non-portable of\n590\t   kind :unresolved-cursor-cells becomes :yin.k/undecodable\n591\t   :unresolved-cursor-cells with the path prefixed by the carried root id;\n592\t   anything else (a resource refusal) propagates as itself; then the root\n593\t   comparison; then 6a's validate-frames, exactly as rows.cljc:576-578. So a\n594\t   numbering defect is reported before a frame defect, as in production.\"\n595\t  [wire]\n596\t  (let [task (with-redefs [rows/validate-frames (constantly nil)]\n597\t               (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false)))\n598\t        root (:yin.k/root wire)\n599\t        id (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n600\t                (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n601\t                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n602\t                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d)) (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n603\t                      (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n604\t                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n605\t                      (throw e)))))]\n606\t    (when (not= id root)\n607\t      (throw (ex-info \"noncanonical\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\n608\t                                      :yin.k/path [root]})))\n609\t    (when (= :task (:kind task))\n610\t      (when-let [d (rows/validate-frames task)]\n611\t        (throw (ex-info \"Frame validation failed\" (update d :yin.k/path #(into [root] %))))))\n612\t    task))\n613\t\n614\t(defn error [f]\n615\t  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\n616\t\n617\t(defn error-brief\n618\t  \"`error` without the probe's counter diagnostic.\"\n619\t  [f]\n620\t  (some-> (error f) (dissoc :probe/used)))\n621\t\n622\t(defn used-at\n623\t  \"Work charged and estimator steps taken at a resource refusal (from the\n624\t   probe diagnostic): estimator steps 0 means the depth scan refused first.\"\n625\t  [f]\n626\t  (let [u (:probe/used (error f))] {:work (:work u) :estimator-steps (:estimator-steps u 0)}))\n627\t\n628\t(defn do-root\n629\t  \"Root id, {:refused ...} for the tie refusal, or {:resource-limit path}.\"\n630\t  [task]\n631\t  (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n632\t       (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n633\t         (let [d (ex-data e)]\n634\t           (case (:yin.k/kind d)\n635\t             :unresolved-cursor-cells {:refused d}\n636\t             :resource-limit {:resource-limit (:yin.k/path d)}\n637\t             (throw e))))))\n638\t\n639\t(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n640\t  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n641\t       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n642\t\n643\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n644\t\n645\t(defn rename-and-shuffle\n646\t  \"Rename every cursor cell by `renaming` and shuffle every :set, :map and\n647\t   the census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers).\"\n648\t  [n renaming]\n649\t  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n650\t                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n651\t                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n652\t                       v))\n653\t          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\n654\t          (go [n]\n655\t            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n656\t              (case kind\n657\t                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\n658\t                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n659\t                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n660\t                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n661\t                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n662\t                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n663\t                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n664\t                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n665\t                                              :else x))\n666\t                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n667\t    (go n)))\n668\t\n669\t(defn reorder\n670\t  \"The task with f applied to the listed members of every :set and :map, in\n671\t   walk order (f gets the container's ordinal and its members, a map's as\n672\t   [k v] pairs); install children included; census untouched.\"\n673\t  [task f]\n674\t  (let [i (atom -1)]\n675\t    (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v) :row [:row (go (second v))] v))\n676\t            (go [n]\n677\t              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n678\t                (case kind\n679\t                  :set (let [k (swap! i inc) ms (mapv value slots)] (rows/node :set (vec (f k ms))))\n680\t                  :map (let [k (swap! i inc) ps (mapv (fn [[a b]] [(value a) (value b)]) (partition 2 slots))]\n681\t                         (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (f k ps)))))\n682\t                  (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n683\t                                          (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n684\t                                                (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n685\t                                                (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n686\t                                                :else x))\n687\t                                        (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n688\t      (go task))))\n689\t\n690\t(defn all-census-cells\n691\t  \"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\"\n692\t  [task]\n693\t  (let [acc (atom [])]\n694\t    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\n695\t                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\n696\t                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\n697\t                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\n698\t                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\n699\t      (node task) (vec @acc))))\n700\t\n701\t(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\n702\t  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\n703\t             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\n704\t             (vec a))))\n705\t\n706\t(defn decoded\n707\t  \"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule).\"\n708\t  [v]\n709\t  (case (first v)\n710\t    :lit [:lit (second v)]\n711\t    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\n712\t    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\n713\t           (case (:kind n)\n714\t             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\n715\t             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\n716\t             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\n717\t             [:row n]))))\n718\t\n719\t(defn distinct-decoded [vs]\n720\t  (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\n721\t\n722\t(defn gen-value\n723\t  \"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded: literals, cursor cells, vectors,\n724\t   sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs (edges,\n725\t   aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\"\n726\t  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n727\t  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n728\t        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\n729\t        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\n730\t    (case k\n731\t      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n732\t      1 (cc)\n733\t      2 (cc)\n734\t      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n735\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\n736\t      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n737\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n738\t      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\n739\t                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n740\t          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n741\t            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n742\t            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\n743\t      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\n744\t              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\n745\t      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\n746\t                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\n747\t                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\n748\t          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\n749\t\n750\t(defn reached [v]\n751\t  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n752\t    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n753\t                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n754\t                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n755\t                     nil))]\n756\t      (w v) @acc)))\n757\t\n758\t(defn gen-task\n759\t  \"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\n760\t   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\"\n761\t  [seed]\n762\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n763\t        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\n764\t        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n765\t        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\n766\t        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n767\t        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n768\t        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n769\t        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n770\t        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n771\t    (if (empty? used)\n772\t      (recur (inc seed))\n773\t      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n774\t        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n775\t\n776\t(defn gen-incidence-task\n777\t  \"The reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families (r3 review, seed-driven):\n778\t   2-8 cells of one body; edge sets, overlapping groups, aliased maps,\n779\t   repeated and nested containers, reversed edges, ordered anchors.\"\n780\t  [seed]\n781\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n782\t        sh (seeded-shuffle r)\n783\t        c (fn [x] [:cursor-cell x])\n784\t        v (fn [xs] [:row (rows/node :vec (vec xs))])\n785\t        s (fn [xs] [:row (rows/node :set (distinct-decoded xs))])\n786\t        m (fn [ps] [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2)))\n787\t                                                                             (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p])) ps)))))])\n788\t        n (+ 2 (.nextInt r 7)) cs (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"i\" %)) (range n))\n789\t        cc #(c (nth cs (.nextInt r n)))\n790\t        edge #(v [(cc) (cc)]) es (vec (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 9)) edge))\n791\t        group #(s (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 4)) cc))\n792\t        value (case (mod seed 10)\n793\t                0 (s es)\n794\t                1 (v [(s es) (s (sh es))])\n795\t                2 (s (repeatedly 4 group))\n796\t                3 (v [(s es) (cc)])\n797\t                4 (s (map #(v [% (group)]) es))\n798\t                5 (m (mapv (fn [x] [(c x) (cc)]) cs))\n799\t                6 (v [(s es) (s (map #(v (reverse (:slots (second %)))) es))])\n800\t                7 (s (map #(m [[(cc) %] [[:lit :anchor] (group)]]) es))\n801\t                8 (s [(v [(s es) (group)]) (v [(group) (s (sh es))])])\n802\t                9 (v [(s es) (s (repeatedly 4 group)) (cc)]))\n803\t        b (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]\n804\t    (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) (sort (reached value))) value)))\n805\t\n806\t(defn worked-act []\n807\t  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\n808\t\n809\t(def park-vector [[:closure 0 [] 2] [:halt 0] [:park 0] [:return 0]])\n810\t(def park-address (jing/segment-key park-vector))\n811\t\n812\t(defn frame-task\n813\t  \"A blocked task carrying the worked code plus a park segment. The wait's\n814\t   act holds `wait-values` in its window register 1 and in one K frame's\n815\t   window register 1; `env-value`, when given, is bound to e0 in the free\n816\t   environment and in every environment; the parked state (P 7) is an act\n817\t   at the park site whose K frame holds `parked-value` in register 1.\n818\t   Decodes under 6a's validate-frames.\"\n819\t  [census wait-values env-value parked-value]\n820\t  (let [base (rt/task-node (conj (vec census) [:test/cursor (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]) nil)\n821\t        [seg pc env window k rd] (:slots (worked-act))\n822\t        [_ store-of] (:slots env)\n823\t        bindings (rows/node :bindings (if env-value ['e0 env-value] []))\n824\t        env (rows/node :env [bindings store-of])\n825\t        kframe (fn [v] (rows/node :kframe [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 v]) rd]))\n826\t        [w1 w2] wait-values\n827\t        wait-act (rows/node :act [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 w1]) (rows/node :k (if w2 [(kframe w2)] [])) rd])\n828\t        pending (second (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame))))\n829\t        parked (rows/node :act [park-address 3 env (rows/node :window []) (rows/node :k [(kframe parked-value)]) 0])]\n830\t    (rows/node :task (-> (:slots base) (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 5 bindings)\n831\t                         (assoc 11 (merge ft/worked-code {park-address park-vector}))\n832\t                         (assoc 8 (rows/node :parked-table (if parked-value [7 parked] [])))\n833\t                         (assoc 13 [(rows/node :wait [wait-act pending])]) (assoc 16 {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork})))))\n834\t\n835\t(defn gen-frame-task [seed]\n836\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n837\t        k (inc (.nextInt r 4))\n838\t        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"f\" %)) (range k))\n839\t        body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n840\t        pick (fn [] (gen-value r cells 2))\n841\t        wait-values (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 2)) pick))\n842\t        env-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n843\t        parked-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n844\t        used (reduce into #{} (map reached (remove nil? (concat wait-values [env-value parked-value]))))]\n845\t    (if (empty? used)\n846\t      (recur (inc seed))\n847\t      (frame-task (mapv #(vector % body) (filter used cells)) wait-values env-value parked-value))))\n848\t\n849\t(defn gen-child-task\n850\t  \"A parent (seed) with two install children: one random child task and the\n851\t   review counterexample as a child; the parent's own values stay random.\"\n852\t  [seed]\n853\t  (let [parent (gen-task seed)\n854\t        child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (gen-task (+ 100000 seed))) 3 :child))]\n855\t    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12 [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n856\t                                                (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))))\n857\t\n858\t(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n859\t  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n860\t  []\n861\t  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n862\t        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n863\t    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n864\t                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n865\t\n866\t(defn with-child\n867\t  \"`parent` with one install 'a holding `child` (slot 3 set to :child).\"\n868\t  [parent child]\n869\t  (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12\n870\t                          [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots child) 3 :child))])])))\n871\t\n872\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics\n873\t\n874\t(defn- permutations [n]\n875\t  (if (zero? n) [[]]\n876\t      (for [p (permutations (dec n)) i (range n)]\n877\t        (vec (concat (subvec p 0 i) [(dec n)] (subvec p i))))))\n878\t\n879\t(defn- factorial [n] (reduce * 1 (range 1 (inc n))))\n880\t\n881\t(defn- container-sizes\n882\t  \"Member count of every :set and :map, indexed by the ordinal reorder gives it.\"\n883\t  [task]\n884\t  (let [acc (atom {})]\n885\t    (reorder task (fn [k ms] (swap! acc assoc k (count ms)) ms))\n886\t    (mapv @acc (range (count @acc)))))\n887\t\n888\t(defn- permissive-root [t]\n889\t  (binding [*numbering-opts* (assoc *numbering-opts* :permissive true)]\n890\t    (let [d (discovery-numbering t)] (first (emit-node* t (:numbering d) {} [])))))\n891\t\n892\t(defn classify-refused\n893\t  \"For a refused state: :interchangeable when every listed order of every\n894\t   set and map gives one root under permissive entry (exhaustive when the\n895\t   number of orders is at most `limit`), :non-interchangeable when two\n896\t   orders differ (exhaustive or sampled), else :undecided (sampled, one\n897\t   root). Permissive entry depends on nothing but listed orders, so the\n898\t   exhaustive verdict is exact.\"\n899\t  [task limit samples]\n900\t  (let [sizes (container-sizes task)\n901\t        total (reduce (fn [t n] (min (* t (factorial n)) 1000000000)) 1 sizes)]\n902\t    (if (<= total limit)\n903\t      (let [choices (reduce (fn [acc n] (for [c acc p (permutations n)] (conj c p))) [[]] sizes)\n904\t            roots (into #{} (map (fn [perms] (permissive-root (reorder task (fn [k ms] (mapv #(nth ms %) (nth perms k))))))) choices)]\n905\t        (if (= 1 (count roots)) :interchangeable :non-interchangeable))"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabb63dd-c047-4e34-a210-01b6c179a82e","uuid":"87558af1-393b-411c-b75c-0e82983a4569","timestamp":"2026-10-12T12:09:38.201Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a/collab/numbering-ref/probe/src/probe/discovery.clj","content":"(ns probe.discovery\n  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering, the BASE design of the\n   note of 2026-10-12 (round 4), over the 6a abstract task representation\n   (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/. The\n   round-3 variants (components, residue search) live in probe.discovery-r3.\n\n   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n\n   The rule. A cursor cell gets the next number the first time the walk meets\n   it. The walk is pre-order over the task in table order. A guest :set or\n   :map is turned into a vector of its members (a map entry as [:entry k v])\n   sorted by KEY: the spelling of a local walk over the member under the\n   current numbering, with numbered cells spelled by number and unnumbered\n   ones by local first-occurrence index, body rank and occurrence count.\n   Members whose keys tie are POSTPONED to the run's queue and re-keyed after\n   the rest of the walk, until a pass numbers nothing. A remaining tie group\n   is entered in listed order when the TIE TEST holds (common key complete;\n   the members' unnumbered cells pairwise disjoint and occurring in no member\n   of another remaining group); otherwise the root walk refuses\n   :yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells and a key walk spells the\n   group as [:tie K ... K] in its key.\n\n   Budgets: :cursor-cells (the census's pair count, before anything is\n   built), :depth (enforced by one bounded iterative scan of the task before\n   any recursive traversal, and again in the walk), :work (one unit per\n   position scanned, visited, keyed or spelled, plus 6a's representation\n   bound of every value before it is CBOR-encoded for a comparison, computed\n   iteratively against the remaining budget). Exceeding one is\n   :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\n\n   Diagnostic switches (*numbering-opts*): :permissive enters a failing group\n   in listed order instead of refusing (used to test interchangeability by\n   exhaustion or sampling); :count-spelling false drops the occurrence count\n   from the key (the owner's literal direction, for measurement).\"\n  (:require [clojure.set :as set]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n\n(def synthetic-table\n  \"The one row that exists only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an\n   ordered pair, so the alias pattern across key and value is in its key.\"\n  {:entry [:V :V]})\n\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n          :else schema)))\n\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n\n(defn bytes-key\n  \"Uncharged encoding; generators and diagnostics only.\"\n  [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n          (= i (count b)) 1\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n\n(defn elem-item\n  \"The item a member is keyed and entered as: a :set member is its V; a :map\n   entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v].\"\n  [kind e]\n  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n\n(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n\n(def default-budget\n  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes and :search; :work now also counts\n   representation bytes, so its default takes over :label-bytes' scale.\"\n  (assoc (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes :search) :work 4194304))\n\n(defn- limit! [ctx category]\n  (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]\n                   ;; probe-only diagnostic (counters at the refusal), not part of the contract\n                   :probe/used @(:counter ctx)})))\n\n(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category)) (limit! ctx category))))\n\n(defn- charge-rep-bound!\n  \"6a's conservative representation bound of x (check-label-bytes!'s `size`:\n   128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length per byte\n   payload, metadata included), charged to :work. Computed with an explicit\n   stack, no recursion, against the budget REMAINING at the call: the running\n   total is checked after every node, so the estimator stops after at most\n   remaining/16 steps on any input and never finishes reading a value it\n   cannot afford. The bound is an abstract charge: an upper bound of the\n   encoding's bytes and of the encoder's intermediate nodes, not a measured\n   host-heap ceiling (the comparison vector and the encoder's intermediates\n   use more heap than the payload length). Reading x allocates the lazy\n   element sequences and `str` of names; nothing proportional to the\n   encoding.\"\n  [ctx x]\n  (let [remaining (- (get (:budget ctx) :work) (get @(:counter ctx) :work 0))]\n    (loop [stack (list (list x)) total 0 steps 0]\n      (if (empty? stack)\n        (do (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) steps)\n            (spend! ctx :work total))\n        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n          (if (empty? s)\n            (recur stack total steps)\n            (let [x (first s)\n                  stack (cons (rest s) stack)\n                  n (cond (cbor/byte-payload? x) (+ 16 (alength ^bytes x))\n                          (string? x) (+ 128 (* 4 (count x)))\n                          (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) (+ 128 (* 4 (count (str x))))\n                          :else 128)\n                  total (+ total n)]\n              (when (> total remaining)\n                (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) (inc steps))\n                (limit! ctx :work))\n              (recur (cond->> stack\n                       (coll? x) (cons (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) (seq x)))\n                       (meta x) (cons (list (meta x))))\n                     total (inc steps)))))))))\n\n(defn- key-bytes!\n  \"Charge the representation bound of x to :work, then encode x for comparison.\n   Every encoding the procedure makes goes through here.\"\n  [ctx x]\n  (charge-rep-bound! ctx x)\n  (let [bs (bytes-key x)]\n    (swap! (:counter ctx) update :encoded-bytes (fnil + 0) (count bs))\n    bs))\n\n(defn- depth-scan!\n  \"The :depth gate, enforced BEFORE any recursive traversal of the task: one\n   bounded pre-order scan with an explicit stack (no recursion) over rows,\n   members, structural and literal data and the census; install children are\n   skipped (each is gated by its own procedure). The depth of a position is\n   the walk's measure, its slot steps from the task (a member step counts\n   one, a map entry's key and value two), plus, inside data, its collection\n   nesting (metadata counts one level). A position deeper than :depth refuses\n   [:depth]; every position costs one :work unit. After the scan every\n   recursive read of the task (cell scans, memo-key hashing, spelling,\n   encoding, 6a's emitter) nests at most :depth levels.\"\n  [ctx task]\n  (let [limit (get (:budget ctx) :depth)\n        node (fn [n k] [:node nil n k])\n        slot (fn [d v k] [:slot d v k])\n        data (fn [v k] [:data nil v k])]\n    ;; The stack holds lazy sequences of entries, so pushing a container's\n    ;; elements is O(1): every position is charged when it is popped, before\n    ;; any work proportional to it is done.\n    (loop [stack (list (list (node task 0)))]\n      (when (seq stack)\n        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n          (if (empty? s)\n            (recur stack)\n            (let [[tag d x k] (first s) stack (cons (rest s) stack)]\n              (spend! ctx :work 1)\n              (when (> k limit) (limit! ctx :depth))\n              (recur\n               (cons\n                (case tag\n                  :node\n                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} x k' (inc k)]\n                    (if (child-task? ctx x)\n                      nil\n                      (let [ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n                        (cond\n                          (= :task kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [i d v]\n                                    (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n                                      (map (fn [child] (node child (inc k'))) v)\n                                      [(slot d v k')]))\n                                  (range) ds slots)\n                          (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[key v]] [(data key k') (slot (second ds) v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          (= :window kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [(data r k') (slot :V v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          (= :set kind) (map (fn [v] (slot :V v k')) slots)\n                          (= :map kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[a b]] [(slot :V a (inc k')) (slot :V b (inc k'))]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          :else (map (fn [d v] (slot d v k')) ds slots)))))\n                  :slot\n                  (cond (ref? d) [(node x k)]\n                        (refs? d) (map (fn [n] (node n (inc k))) x)\n                        (= :C d) nil\n                        (v? d) (when x\n                                 (case (first x)\n                                   :cursor-cell nil\n                                   :row [(node (second x) k)]\n                                   :lit [(data (second x) k)]\n                                   [(data x k)]))\n                        :else [(data x k)])\n                  :data\n                  (concat (when (coll? x) (map (fn [e] (data e (inc k))) (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) x)))\n                          (when (meta x) [(data (meta x) (inc k))])))\n                stack)))))))))\n\n(defn- sort-by-key!\n  \"xs in the bytewise order of (f x), each encoded once (stable).\"\n  [ctx f xs]\n  (mapv second (sort #(compare-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) (mapv (fn [x] [(key-bytes! ctx (f x)) x]) xs))))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ cell scans\n\n(defn- walk-cells\n  \"Call f on every cursor cell at a position under slot (d x); census and\n   install children excluded; one :work unit per position.\"\n  [ctx d x f]\n  (letfn [(slot [d x]\n            (spend! ctx :work 1)\n            (cond (ref? d) (node x)\n                  (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                  (= :C d) (f x)\n                  (v? d) (when x (case (first x) :cursor-cell (f (second x)) :row (node (second x)) nil))\n                  :else nil))\n          (node [n]\n            (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (child-task? ctx n))\n              (dorun (map slot (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n)))))]\n    (slot d x)))\n\n(defn item-cells\n  \"The cursor cells under slot (d x), memoized per procedure on the slot value.\"\n  [ctx d x]\n  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\n      (let [acc (atom #{})]\n        (walk-cells ctx d x #(swap! acc conj %))\n        (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\n        @acc)))\n\n(defn- occurrence-counts\n  \"Cursor cell -> number of positions of the task holding it.\"\n  [ctx task]\n  (let [acc (atom {})]\n    (walk-cells ctx [:ref :task] task #(swap! acc update % (fnil inc 0)))\n    @acc))\n\n(defn body-label [body]\n  (let [[stream position] (:slots body)]\n    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n\n(defn- body-ranks\n  \"Cursor cell -> rank of its body label among the census's distinct body\n   labels in bytewise order (rank order = label order, so keys stay\n   independent of the descriptor's size).\"\n  [ctx bodies]\n  (let [labels (into {} (map (fn [[c b]] [c (body-label b)])) bodies)\n        ranks (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i l] [l i])) (sort-by-key! ctx identity (distinct (vals labels))))]\n    (into {} (map (fn [[c l]] [c (get ranks l)])) labels)))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\n\n(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\n\n(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next]\n  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next) :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\n\n(defn- meet!\n  \"W2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\"\n  [run c]\n  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\n    (let [k @(:next run)]\n      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\n      (swap! (:next run) inc)\n      true)))\n\n(declare enter-node! unordered! local-run spell-slot spell-node)\n\n(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run)]\n    (spend! ctx :work 1)\n    (when (> (count path) (get (:budget ctx) :depth)) (limit! ctx :depth))\n    (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\n          (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\n          (= :C d) (meet! run x)\n          (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n                           :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\n                           :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\n                           nil))\n          :else nil)))\n\n(defn- enter-node!\n  \"W1. Pre-order: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in order;\n   keyed structural rows in the byte order of the key; :window in register\n   order; installs and module stores by name; :set and :map by keys with\n   ties postponed; census and children not entered.\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n      (child-task? ctx n) nil\n      (= :task kind)\n      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n          (doseq [child (sort-by-key! ctx #(first (:slots %)) x)]\n            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\n          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (doseq [[k v] (sort-by-key! ctx first (partition 2 slots))]\n        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\n      (= :set kind) (unordered! run n :set (vec slots) path)\n      (= :map kind) (unordered! run n :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\n      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n(defn- unordered!\n  \"The transformation: key every member by a local walk under the numbering\n   at this moment; members with a unique key are entered in key order; a run\n   of equal keys is one tie group, postponed to the run's queue (postponement\n   order: containers in walk order, groups within a container in key order).\n   A member's path is the container's path plus :member (there is no\n   canonical index into an unordered container); a group remembers its\n   container by identity.\"\n  [run container kind elems path]\n  (let [keyed (mapv (fn [e]\n                      ;; one unit per member keyed, so the number of key walks\n                      ;; (and of memo lookups, each a read of charged data) is charged\n                      (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n                      [(local-run run (elem-item kind e) (conj path :member)) e]) elems)\n        groups (partition-by (comp :bytes first) (sort #(compare-bytes (:bytes (first %1)) (:bytes (first %2))) keyed))]\n    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n      (println \"  [sort]\" path \"num\" @(:num run))\n      (doseq [g groups] (println \"    \" (if (= 1 (count g)) \"enter\" \"POSTPONE\") (mapv (fn [[r e]] [e (:spell r)]) g))))\n    (doseq [g groups]\n      (if (= 1 (count g))\n        (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind (second (first g))) (conj path :member))\n        (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :container container :kind kind :path path\n                                   :key (:spell (first (first g))) :complete? (:complete? (first (first g)))\n                                   :elems (mapv second g)})))))\n\n(defn- fixpoint!\n  \"W3. Re-examine every postponed group in order under the current numbering;\n   repeat while a pass numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\n      (reset! (:groups run) [])\n      (doseq [{:keys [container kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run container kind elems path))\n      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\n\n(defn- unnumbered-cells [run kind e]\n  (let [num @(:num run)]\n    (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells (:ctx run) :V (elem-item kind e))))))\n\n(defn- group-failure\n  \"T, the tie test. nil when the group's members are interchangeable: the\n   common key is complete, and the members' unnumbered cursor cells are\n   pairwise disjoint and occur in no member of another remaining group.\n   Otherwise the reason.\"\n  [run groups g]\n  (let [own (mapv #(unnumbered-cells run (:kind g) %) (:elems g))\n        others (reduce into #{} (for [h groups :when (not= (:id h) (:id g)) e (:elems h)]\n                                  (unnumbered-cells run (:kind h) e)))]\n    (cond (not (:complete? g)) :incomplete-key\n          (not= (reduce + (map count own)) (count (reduce into #{} own))) :shared-within-group\n          (some #(seq (set/intersection % others)) own) :shared-across-groups\n          :else nil)))\n\n(defn- resolve!\n  \"Postponed groups to completion: fixpoint, then the tie test on every\n   remaining group; all pass: enter them in listed order and repeat; any\n   fails: the root run refuses, a key run leaves them as residue.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (fixpoint! run)\n    (let [groups @(:groups run) ctx (:ctx run)]\n      (when (seq groups)\n        (let [failing (vec (keep (fn [g] (when-let [why (group-failure run groups g)] (assoc g :why why))) groups))]\n          (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n            (println \"  [ties]\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (:elems g) (:why g)]) (map #(assoc % :why (group-failure run groups %)) groups))))\n          (cond\n            (empty? failing)\n            ;; Interchangeable groups are independent of each other (their\n            ;; cells are confined), so all are entered before the next pass.\n            (do (reset! (:groups run) [])\n                (doseq [g groups e (:elems g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item (:kind g) e) (conj (:path g) :member)))\n                (recur))\n            (:permissive ctx)\n            ;; Diagnostic only: enter the first group in listed order, then\n            ;; re-key the rest (what an unsound \"equal keys, host order\" rule does).\n            (do (reset! (:groups run) (vec (rest groups)))\n                (doseq [e (:elems (first groups))] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item (:kind (first groups)) e) (conj (:path (first groups)) :member)))\n                (recur))\n            (= :n (:tag run))\n            (reset! (:refused run)\n                    {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                     :path (:path (first failing))\n                     :why (mapv :why failing)\n                     :cursor-cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat (fn [g] (mapcat #(unnumbered-cells run (:kind g) %) (:elems g))) failing))))})\n            :else nil))))))\n\n(defn- local-run\n  \"A key walk: the same procedure on one member from the enclosing run's\n   numbering, tag :u; its result is the member's key (spelling under its\n   final numbering, residue spelled [:tie K ...]) and whether it is complete.\n   Memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the item's cells, counter).\"\n  [run item path]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\n        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\n        num @(:num run)\n        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\n        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start]]\n    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\n        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start)]\n          (spend! ctx :work (count cells))\n          (enter-slot! sub :V item path)\n          (resolve! sub)\n          (let [spell (spell-slot sub :V item path)\n                r {:spell spell :bytes (key-bytes! ctx spell) :complete? (empty? @(:groups sub))}]\n            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :local-runs (fnil + 0) 1)\n            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\n            r)))))\n\n;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\n\n(defn- spell-cell [run c]\n  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c) ctx (:ctx run)]\n    (case t\n      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\n      :u [:cursor-cell (if (:count-spelling ctx)\n                         [:u k (get (:ranks ctx) c) (get (:counts ctx) c)]\n                         [:u k (get (:ranks ctx) c)])]\n      (throw (ex-info \"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\n\n(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\n  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n                   (case (first x)\n                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\n                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\n                     x))\n        :else x))\n\n(defn- spell-node\n  \"The spelling of a row under the run's final numbering: literals and\n   structural data as themselves (by reference), rows expanded in place,\n   keyed rows in key order, a :set or :map as the sorted vector of its\n   members' spellings with each surviving tie group as [:tie K ...].\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\n      (= :task kind)\n      (into [kind]\n            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\n                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n                             (sort-by-key! ctx #(nth % 1) (mapv #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x))\n                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\n                         (map vector ds slots)))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (sort-by-key! ctx first\n                                                  (mapv (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\n                                                        (partition 2 slots)))))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\n      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n            tied (filter #(identical? n (:container %)) @(:groups run))\n            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\n            plain (keep (fn [e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path :member)))) elems)\n            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\n        (into [kind] (sort-by-key! ctx identity (concat plain ties))))\n      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\n\n(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts*\n  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install\n   children): :permissive, :count-spelling, :budget.\"\n  {})\n\n(defn discovery-numbering\n  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n   Throws :resource-limit when a budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n  ([task opts]\n   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n         ctx {:task task\n              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n              :counter (atom {})\n              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n              :count-spelling (not= false (:count-spelling opts))}\n         ;; gates first: the census's pair count before the bodies map is\n         ;; built, the depth scan before any recursive traversal\n         _ (spend! ctx :cursor-cells (quot (count (:slots census)) 2))\n         _ (depth-scan! ctx task)\n         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n         ctx (assoc ctx :bodies bodies)\n         ctx (assoc ctx :ranks (body-ranks ctx bodies) :counts (occurrence-counts ctx task))\n         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1)]\n     (enter-node! run task [])\n     (resolve! run)\n     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n         (when (not= numbered declared)\n           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (sort (remove numbered declared)))\n                                                      :extra (vec (sort (remove declared numbered)))})))\n         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n\n(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n\n(declare emit-node*)\n\n(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\n  (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)\n                   ;; probe-only diagnostic, not part of the refusal's contract\n                   :probe/why (:why r)})))\n\n(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows path]\n  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n  (cond\n    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows path)\n    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] [j n]]\n                        (let [p (conj path (if (= :install (:kind n)) (first (:slots n)) j))\n                              [id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs p)]\n                          [(conj out id) rs']))\n                      [[] rows] (map-indexed vector x))\n    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n    (v? d) (case (first x)\n             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows path)] [[:row id] rs])\n             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n             [x rows])\n    :else [x rows]))\n\n(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [i d x]]\n                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x)\n                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) path))\n                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {} [])]\n                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs (conj path i))] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n                             [[] rows] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n        slots (if (= kind :task)\n                (-> slots\n                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n                (ordered-slots kind slots))\n        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\n    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\n\n(defn emit*\n  \"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\n   :unresolved-cursor-cells for the root or for any install child (path\n   [12 <name> ...]); a budget refusal propagates as itself.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\n    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {} [])]\n      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\n\n(defn decode*\n  \"Receiver side, in the production order (rows.cljc:570-578): 6a's decode*\n   steps 1-5 with its own numbering check off and its frame validation\n   suppressed (it still runs 6a's recompute under 6a's budget: a probe\n   limitation), then the whole discovery recomputation (root and install\n   children) under one translation: only a lift-side :yin.k/non-portable of\n   kind :unresolved-cursor-cells becomes :yin.k/undecodable\n   :unresolved-cursor-cells with the path prefixed by the carried root id;\n   anything else (a resource refusal) propagates as itself; then the root\n   comparison; then 6a's validate-frames, exactly as rows.cljc:576-578. So a\n   numbering defect is reported before a frame defect, as in production.\"\n  [wire]\n  (let [task (with-redefs [rows/validate-frames (constantly nil)]\n               (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false)))\n        root (:yin.k/root wire)\n        id (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n                (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d)) (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n                      (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n                      (throw e)))))]\n    (when (not= id root)\n      (throw (ex-info \"noncanonical\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\n                                      :yin.k/path [root]})))\n    (when (= :task (:kind task))\n      (when-let [d (rows/validate-frames task)]\n        (throw (ex-info \"Frame validation failed\" (update d :yin.k/path #(into [root] %))))))\n    task))\n\n(defn error [f]\n  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\n\n(defn error-brief\n  \"`error` without the probe's counter diagnostic.\"\n  [f]\n  (some-> (error f) (dissoc :probe/used)))\n\n(defn used-at\n  \"Work charged and estimator steps taken at a resource refusal (from the\n   probe diagnostic): estimator steps 0 means the depth scan refused first.\"\n  [f]\n  (let [u (:probe/used (error f))] {:work (:work u) :estimator-steps (:estimator-steps u 0)}))\n\n(defn do-root\n  \"Root id, {:refused ...} for the tie refusal, or {:resource-limit path}.\"\n  [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n       (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n         (let [d (ex-data e)]\n           (case (:yin.k/kind d)\n             :unresolved-cursor-cells {:refused d}\n             :resource-limit {:resource-limit (:yin.k/path d)}\n             (throw e))))))\n\n(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n\n(defn rename-and-shuffle\n  \"Rename every cursor cell by `renaming` and shuffle every :set, :map and\n   the census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers).\"\n  [n renaming]\n  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n                       v))\n          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\n          (go [n]\n            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n              (case kind\n                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\n                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n                                              :else x))\n                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n    (go n)))\n\n(defn reorder\n  \"The task with f applied to the listed members of every :set and :map, in\n   walk order (f gets the container's ordinal and its members, a map's as\n   [k v] pairs); install children included; census untouched.\"\n  [task f]\n  (let [i (atom -1)]\n    (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v) :row [:row (go (second v))] v))\n            (go [n]\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n                (case kind\n                  :set (let [k (swap! i inc) ms (mapv value slots)] (rows/node :set (vec (f k ms))))\n                  :map (let [k (swap! i inc) ps (mapv (fn [[a b]] [(value a) (value b)]) (partition 2 slots))]\n                         (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (f k ps)))))\n                  (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n                                          (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n                                                (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n                                                (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n                                                :else x))\n                                        (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n      (go task))))\n\n(defn all-census-cells\n  \"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\"\n  [task]\n  (let [acc (atom [])]\n    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\n                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\n                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\n                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\n      (node task) (vec @acc))))\n\n(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\n  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\n             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\n             (vec a))))\n\n(defn decoded\n  \"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule).\"\n  [v]\n  (case (first v)\n    :lit [:lit (second v)]\n    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\n    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\n           (case (:kind n)\n             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\n             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\n             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\n             [:row n]))))\n\n(defn distinct-decoded [vs]\n  (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\n\n(defn gen-value\n  \"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded: literals, cursor cells, vectors,\n   sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs (edges,\n   aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\"\n  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\n        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\n    (case k\n      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n      1 (cc)\n      2 (cc)\n      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\n      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\n                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\n      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\n              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\n      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\n                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\n                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\n          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\n\n(defn reached [v]\n  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n                     nil))]\n      (w v) @acc)))\n\n(defn gen-task\n  \"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\n   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\n        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n\n(defn gen-incidence-task\n  \"The reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families (r3 review, seed-driven):\n   2-8 cells of one body; edge sets, overlapping groups, aliased maps,\n   repeated and nested containers, reversed edges, ordered anchors.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        sh (seeded-shuffle r)\n        c (fn [x] [:cursor-cell x])\n        v (fn [xs] [:row (rows/node :vec (vec xs))])\n        s (fn [xs] [:row (rows/node :set (distinct-decoded xs))])\n        m (fn [ps] [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2)))\n                                                                             (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p])) ps)))))])\n        n (+ 2 (.nextInt r 7)) cs (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"i\" %)) (range n))\n        cc #(c (nth cs (.nextInt r n)))\n        edge #(v [(cc) (cc)]) es (vec (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 9)) edge))\n        group #(s (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 4)) cc))\n        value (case (mod seed 10)\n                0 (s es)\n                1 (v [(s es) (s (sh es))])\n                2 (s (repeatedly 4 group))\n                3 (v [(s es) (cc)])\n                4 (s (map #(v [% (group)]) es))\n                5 (m (mapv (fn [x] [(c x) (cc)]) cs))\n                6 (v [(s es) (s (map #(v (reverse (:slots (second %)))) es))])\n                7 (s (map #(m [[(cc) %] [[:lit :anchor] (group)]]) es))\n                8 (s [(v [(s es) (group)]) (v [(group) (s (sh es))])])\n                9 (v [(s es) (s (repeatedly 4 group)) (cc)]))\n        b (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]\n    (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) (sort (reached value))) value)))\n\n(defn worked-act []\n  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\n\n(def park-vector [[:closure 0 [] 2] [:halt 0] [:park 0] [:return 0]])\n(def park-address (jing/segment-key park-vector))\n\n(defn frame-task\n  \"A blocked task carrying the worked code plus a park segment. The wait's\n   act holds `wait-values` in its window register 1 and in one K frame's\n   window register 1; `env-value`, when given, is bound to e0 in the free\n   environment and in every environment; the parked state (P 7) is an act\n   at the park site whose K frame holds `parked-value` in register 1.\n   Decodes under 6a's validate-frames.\"\n  [census wait-values env-value parked-value]\n  (let [base (rt/task-node (conj (vec census) [:test/cursor (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]) nil)\n        [seg pc env window k rd] (:slots (worked-act))\n        [_ store-of] (:slots env)\n        bindings (rows/node :bindings (if env-value ['e0 env-value] []))\n        env (rows/node :env [bindings store-of])\n        kframe (fn [v] (rows/node :kframe [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 v]) rd]))\n        [w1 w2] wait-values\n        wait-act (rows/node :act [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 w1]) (rows/node :k (if w2 [(kframe w2)] [])) rd])\n        pending (second (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame))))\n        parked (rows/node :act [park-address 3 env (rows/node :window []) (rows/node :k [(kframe parked-value)]) 0])]\n    (rows/node :task (-> (:slots base) (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 5 bindings)\n                         (assoc 11 (merge ft/worked-code {park-address park-vector}))\n                         (assoc 8 (rows/node :parked-table (if parked-value [7 parked] [])))\n                         (assoc 13 [(rows/node :wait [wait-act pending])]) (assoc 16 {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork})))))\n\n(defn gen-frame-task [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 4))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"f\" %)) (range k))\n        body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        pick (fn [] (gen-value r cells 2))\n        wait-values (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 2)) pick))\n        env-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        parked-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        used (reduce into #{} (map reached (remove nil? (concat wait-values [env-value parked-value]))))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (frame-task (mapv #(vector % body) (filter used cells)) wait-values env-value parked-value))))\n\n(defn gen-child-task\n  \"A parent (seed) with two install children: one random child task and the\n   review counterexample as a child; the parent's own values stay random.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [parent (gen-task seed)\n        child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (gen-task (+ 100000 seed))) 3 :child))]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12 [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n                                                (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))))\n\n(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n  []\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n\n(defn with-child\n  \"`parent` with one install 'a holding `child` (slot 3 set to :child).\"\n  [parent child]\n  (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12\n                          [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots child) 3 :child))])])))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics\n\n(defn- permutations [n]\n  (if (zero? n) [[]]\n      (for [p (permutations (dec n)) i (range n)]\n        (vec (concat (subvec p 0 i) [(dec n)] (subvec p i))))))\n\n(defn- factorial [n] (reduce * 1 (range 1 (inc n))))\n\n(defn- container-sizes\n  \"Member count of every :set and :map, indexed by the ordinal reorder gives it.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [acc (atom {})]\n    (reorder task (fn [k ms] (swap! acc assoc k (count ms)) ms))\n    (mapv @acc (range (count @acc)))))\n\n(defn- permissive-root [t]\n  (binding [*numbering-opts* (assoc *numbering-opts* :permissive true)]\n    (let [d (discovery-numbering t)] (first (emit-node* t (:numbering d) {} [])))))\n\n(defn classify-refused\n  \"For a refused state: :interchangeable when every listed order of every\n   set and map gives one root under permissive entry (exhaustive when the\n   number of orders is at most `limit`), :non-interchangeable when two\n   orders differ (exhaustive or sampled), else :undecided (sampled, one\n   root). Permissive entry depends on nothing but listed orders, so the\n   exhaustive verdict is exact.\"\n  [task limit samples]\n  (let [sizes (container-sizes task)\n        total (reduce (fn [t n] (min (* t (factorial n)) 1000000000)) 1 sizes)]\n    (if (<= total limit)\n      (let [choices (reduce (fn [acc n] (for [c acc p (permutations n)] (conj c p))) [[]] sizes)\n            roots (into #{} (map (fn [perms] (permissive-root (reorder task (fn [k ms] (mapv #(nth ms %) (nth perms k))))))) choices)]\n        (if (= 1 (count roots)) :interchangeable :non-interchangeable))","numLines":90