{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UM6nf4FAVhHJGqqSDuQVgb","type":"tool_result","content":"440\t\n441\tThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n442\tempty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\n443\tnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n444\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n445\tunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n446\t\n447\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n448\taddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n449\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n450\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n451\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n452\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n453\t\n454\t## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n455\t\n456\tCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n457\ttwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\n458\teverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n459\telsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n460\tholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n461\tlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n462\tcan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n463\ttopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n464\twhich any algorithm is checked.\n465\t\n466\t**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\n467\tis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\n468\tof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\n469\tcursor cell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cursor cells of\n470\tthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\n471\tminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\n472\tdefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\n473\tit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\n474\tcursor cells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n475\t(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\n476\tequal-body cursor cells leaves all seven cursor cells in one class, and no\n477\tautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\n478\tprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\n479\tpruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n480\tprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n481\tsymmetry argument.\n482\t\n483\t**The procedure.** Its input is the abstract task: the structure of\n484\t§5 with cursor cells as identities, before any numbering. Every encoding below\n485\tis canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) of a *synthetic vector\n486\tstructure*; vectors are used throughout, never host maps or sets, so\n487\tthat elements or keys that become equal after class substitution keep\n488\ttheir multiplicity. All byte comparisons are bytewise on those CBOR\n489\tbytes. Let the cursor cells be `n` in number; if `n = 0` the procedure returns\n490\tthe empty numbering and performs no search.\n491\t\n492\t1. **Class labels** are CBOR byte strings. Wherever a label is embedded\n493\t   in a larger encoding it is embedded as a CBOR byte string (major\n494\t   type 2), never re-parsed. A cursor cell's initial label is the CBOR of\n495\t   `[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`.\n496\t2. **Class encoding** `CE(x)` of any encoded value or row, structural\n497\t   and hash-free. **Every identity-bearing position encodes the cursor cell's\n498\t   current label, never its incoming id**; the identity positions are\n499\t   exactly: `[:cursor-cell C]` values, the bare `cursor-cell-C` and `response-cursor-cell-C`\n500\t   slots of `:pending` rows, and the keys of the `:cursor-cells` census.\n501\t   - `[:lit d]` → `[:lit d]`.\n502\t   - `[:cursor-cell C]`, and a bare `C` slot → `[:cursor-cell <label of C>]` (full\n503\t     tag retained, §10.9).\n504\t   - `[:row B]` → `[:row <CE(row B)>]`, the row expanded in place; the\n505\t     expansion is finite because the structural edge family is acyclic\n506\t     (§8 step 3).\n507\t   - A row `[kind s₁ … sₖ]` → `[kind CE(s₁) … CE(sₖ)]` with each slot\n508\t     encoded as: a structural datum (`A`, `M`, `P`, `S`, `N`, `D`, a\n509\t     reason, a phase, a version, a role, a kind) as itself; a `C` as\n510\t     above; a `V` as above; a cref as the expanded row; a crefs vector\n511\t     as the vector of expansions.\n512\t   - For `:set` rows, and for the key-value pairs of `:map`, `:store`\n513\t     and `:bindings` rows, the entries of `:parked-table`, and the\n514\t     installs and module-stores slots of the root: encode each element\n515\t     (or each `[key value]` pair as a two-element vector), then **sort\n516\t     the resulting vector of encodings bytewise, retaining duplicates**.\n517\t     Ordered rows (`:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) and the ordered\n518\t     `frames` slot keep their order.\n519\t   - The `:cursor-cells` census is **not expanded**: in `CE(root)` the\n520\t     `cursor-cells-cref` slot is the constant `[:cursor-cells]`. The census is a\n521\t     derived index of the cursor cells the task reaches, so it carries no\n522\t     additional information beyond the reached cursor cell identities and\n523\t     their bodies (which already seed the initial labels), and\n524\t     (consistently with\n525\t     §8 step 3, which excludes census declaration edges from\n526\t     reachability) **census declaration occurrences contribute no\n527\t     positions** to any cursor cell.\n528\t   - Context lookups (`P` in `:parked-ref` and `parked-id`, `M` in\n529\t     `store-of`) are not expanded; they appear as the plain datum.\n530\t   - An install child's `:task` row is **not expanded**: children are\n531\t     numbered first, as their own tasks with their own cursor cells (task\n532\t     boundary; this runs bottom-up inside §8 step 6 and needs no\n533\t     restoration), and a child cref appears in the parent's encodings\n534\t     as `[:child <child root id>]`, the child's already-canonical root\n535\t     id as a byte string.\n536\t3. **Positions.** A position is the CBOR of the path from the task's\n537\t   root to one identity-bearing occurrence of a cursor cell: a vector of\n538\t   steps, built by descending from the root and appending exactly one\n539\t   step per slot category crossed, with the occurrence itself\n540\t   contributing no step. The categories, and their one spelling each:\n541\t   - *Fixed slot* (any tabled slot that is not one of the categories\n542\t     below, including a `:pending` row's `cursor-cell-C` slots):\n543\t     `[kind slot-index]`, the slot's 0-based index in the row table.\n544\t   - *Ordered repeated entry* (`:vec`, `:list` elements; `:k`\n545\t     frames; the root's `frames`): `[kind i]` with `i` the 0-based\n546\t     position; for `frames` the step is `[:task :frame i]`.\n547\t   - *Unordered element* (`:set`): `[kind :elem CE(e)]`; never an index.\n548\t   - *Keyed pair* (`:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, and\n549\t     the root's installs and module-stores slots, keyed by module name\n550\t     and `M`): `[kind :key CE(k)]` when descending into the key,\n551\t     `[kind :val CE(k)]` when descending into the value; never an\n552\t     index. For `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, installs and module\n553\t     stores the key is a structural datum and `CE(k)` is the datum.\n554\t   - *Task boundary*: a path never crosses into an install child; a\n555\t     child's cursor cells have paths rooted at the child's own root.\n556\t   Equal occurrences yield equal positions, one per occurrence, and\n557\t   positions never contain a source enumeration index of an unordered\n558\t   container.\n559\t4. **Signature and refinement.** A cursor cell's signature is the CBOR of\n560\t   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n561\t   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n562\t   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n563\t   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n564\t   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n565\t   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n566\t5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n567\t   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n568\t   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n569\t   give that member the label that is the CBOR byte string of\n570\t   `[:ind <depth> <old label>]`, where `depth` is 0 at the top-level\n571\t   search and increases by one per level of recursion (distinct from\n572\t   every refinement label by its tag; no ordering relative to other\n573\t   labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n574\t   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n575\t   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n576\t   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n577\t   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n578\t   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n579\t   identical rows: they differ only in which source cursor cell received\n580\t   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n581\t\n582\t**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\n583\ta source-cursor-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\n584\tnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\n585\tprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\n586\tcarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n587\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\n588\tcommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\n589\tdecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\n590\tkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n591\tprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\n592\tany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\n593\tfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n594\t\n595\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n596\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cursor cell may number it\n597\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n598\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n599\tnever affected rows below a cursor cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n600\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cursor-cell C]`\n601\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cursor cell share it and one\n602\treference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n603\t\n604\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n605\t\n606\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n607\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\n608\tid through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\n609\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabb63dd-c047-4e34-a210-01b6c179a82e","uuid":"591f0dbc-24e8-4743-aef1-a571ba575118","timestamp":"2026-10-12T12:35:19.372Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a/collab/1792250000000-carrier.master.md","content":"\nThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\nempty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\nnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\nunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n\nThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\naddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\nare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\nplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\nkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\nthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n\n## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n\nCursor-cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\ntwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cursor cell's number must depend on\neverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\nelsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\nholding cursor cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\nlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\ncan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\ntopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\nwhich any algorithm is checked.\n\n**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\nis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\nof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\ncursor cell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cursor cells of\nthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\nminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\ndefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\nit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\ncursor cells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\nequal-body cursor cells leaves all seven cursor cells in one class, and no\nautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\nprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\npruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\nprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\nsymmetry argument.\n\n**The procedure.** Its input is the abstract task: the structure of\n§5 with cursor cells as identities, before any numbering. Every encoding below\nis canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) of a *synthetic vector\nstructure*; vectors are used throughout, never host maps or sets, so\nthat elements or keys that become equal after class substitution keep\ntheir multiplicity. All byte comparisons are bytewise on those CBOR\nbytes. Let the cursor cells be `n` in number; if `n = 0` the procedure returns\nthe empty numbering and performs no search.\n\n1. **Class labels** are CBOR byte strings. Wherever a label is embedded\n   in a larger encoding it is embedded as a CBOR byte string (major\n   type 2), never re-parsed. A cursor cell's initial label is the CBOR of\n   `[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`.\n2. **Class encoding** `CE(x)` of any encoded value or row, structural\n   and hash-free. **Every identity-bearing position encodes the cursor cell's\n   current label, never its incoming id**; the identity positions are\n   exactly: `[:cursor-cell C]` values, the bare `cursor-cell-C` and `response-cursor-cell-C`\n   slots of `:pending` rows, and the keys of the `:cursor-cells` census.\n   - `[:lit d]` → `[:lit d]`.\n   - `[:cursor-cell C]`, and a bare `C` slot → `[:cursor-cell <label of C>]` (full\n     tag retained, §10.9).\n   - `[:row B]` → `[:row <CE(row B)>]`, the row expanded in place; the\n     expansion is finite because the structural edge family is acyclic\n     (§8 step 3).\n   - A row `[kind s₁ … sₖ]` → `[kind CE(s₁) … CE(sₖ)]` with each slot\n     encoded as: a structural datum (`A`, `M`, `P`, `S`, `N`, `D`, a\n     reason, a phase, a version, a role, a kind) as itself; a `C` as\n     above; a `V` as above; a cref as the expanded row; a crefs vector\n     as the vector of expansions.\n   - For `:set` rows, and for the key-value pairs of `:map`, `:store`\n     and `:bindings` rows, the entries of `:parked-table`, and the\n     installs and module-stores slots of the root: encode each element\n     (or each `[key value]` pair as a two-element vector), then **sort\n     the resulting vector of encodings bytewise, retaining duplicates**.\n     Ordered rows (`:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) and the ordered\n     `frames` slot keep their order.\n   - The `:cursor-cells` census is **not expanded**: in `CE(root)` the\n     `cursor-cells-cref` slot is the constant `[:cursor-cells]`. The census is a\n     derived index of the cursor cells the task reaches, so it carries no\n     additional information beyond the reached cursor cell identities and\n     their bodies (which already seed the initial labels), and\n     (consistently with\n     §8 step 3, which excludes census declaration edges from\n     reachability) **census declaration occurrences contribute no\n     positions** to any cursor cell.\n   - Context lookups (`P` in `:parked-ref` and `parked-id`, `M` in\n     `store-of`) are not expanded; they appear as the plain datum.\n   - An install child's `:task` row is **not expanded**: children are\n     numbered first, as their own tasks with their own cursor cells (task\n     boundary; this runs bottom-up inside §8 step 6 and needs no\n     restoration), and a child cref appears in the parent's encodings\n     as `[:child <child root id>]`, the child's already-canonical root\n     id as a byte string.\n3. **Positions.** A position is the CBOR of the path from the task's\n   root to one identity-bearing occurrence of a cursor cell: a vector of\n   steps, built by descending from the root and appending exactly one\n   step per slot category crossed, with the occurrence itself\n   contributing no step. The categories, and their one spelling each:\n   - *Fixed slot* (any tabled slot that is not one of the categories\n     below, including a `:pending` row's `cursor-cell-C` slots):\n     `[kind slot-index]`, the slot's 0-based index in the row table.\n   - *Ordered repeated entry* (`:vec`, `:list` elements; `:k`\n     frames; the root's `frames`): `[kind i]` with `i` the 0-based\n     position; for `frames` the step is `[:task :frame i]`.\n   - *Unordered element* (`:set`): `[kind :elem CE(e)]`; never an index.\n   - *Keyed pair* (`:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, and\n     the root's installs and module-stores slots, keyed by module name\n     and `M`): `[kind :key CE(k)]` when descending into the key,\n     `[kind :val CE(k)]` when descending into the value; never an\n     index. For `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, installs and module\n     stores the key is a structural datum and `CE(k)` is the datum.\n   - *Task boundary*: a path never crosses into an install child; a\n     child's cursor cells have paths rooted at the child's own root.\n   Equal occurrences yield equal positions, one per occurrence, and\n   positions never contain a source enumeration index of an unordered\n   container.\n4. **Signature and refinement.** A cursor cell's signature is the CBOR of\n   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   give that member the label that is the CBOR byte string of\n   `[:ind <depth> <old label>]`, where `depth` is 0 at the top-level\n   search and increases by one per level of recursion (distinct from\n   every refinement label by its tag; no ordering relative to other\n   labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   identical rows: they differ only in which source cursor cell received\n   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n\n**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\na source-cursor-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\nnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\nprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\ncarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\ncommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\ndecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\nany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\nfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n\nUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\ndifferent states sharing a closure over the same cursor cell may number it\ndifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\ncross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\nnever affected rows below a cursor cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\nprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cursor-cell C]`\nmaps to that entry; two references to one cursor cell share it and one\nreference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n\n## 7. Equality and the content address\n\nThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\nthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\nid through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\ncensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,","numLines":170,"startLine":440,"totalLines":868}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UM6nf4FAVhHJGqqSDuQVgb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
